(window.SERVICE_BANKS = window.SERVICE_BANKS || {})["aoai"] = {"name":"Azure OpenAI & Foundry","icon":"🤖","blurb":"Azure OpenAI in Microsoft Foundry from A to Z: resources, models, deployments and quota, security, the Chat Completions and Responses APIs, tools, embeddings, prompts, content safety, RAG with Azure AI Search, fine-tuning, monitoring, and calling models from SQL.","order":1,"sourceNote":"Written from the Microsoft Learn documentation; every explanation links the page it was checked against.","sections":[{"id":"1","name":"Foundry platform and resources"},{"id":"2","name":"Models, versions and lifecycle"},{"id":"3","name":"Deployments, quota and throughput"},{"id":"4","name":"Authentication, access, networking and privacy"},{"id":"5","name":"The APIs: v1, Chat Completions, Responses and reasoning"},{"id":"6","name":"Function calling and output control"},{"id":"7","name":"Embeddings"},{"id":"8","name":"Prompt engineering and system messages"},{"id":"9","name":"Content safety and guardrails"},{"id":"10","name":"RAG and Azure AI Search (Foundry IQ)"},{"id":"11","name":"Fine-tuning, evaluation, monitoring and agents"},{"id":"12","name":"Calling Azure OpenAI from SQL"}],"questions":[{"id":"aoai-1.1-1","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.1 · What Microsoft Foundry is","scenario":"","stem":"Your team still uses the names from older Azure AI documentation. Which current name replaces **Azure AI Studio / Azure AI Foundry**?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Azure AI Studio, then Azure AI Foundry, is now **Microsoft Foundry**. **Azure AI Services** became **Foundry Tools** (Speech, Language, Vision and so on), and **Foundry IQ** is the new name for Azure AI Search as a knowledge source.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Microsoft Foundry:** the portal at ai.azure.com, its resources and projects. Old docs that say *\"open Azure AI Foundry\"* now mean this.\n- **Foundry Tools** is the new name for **Azure AI Services**: the prebuilt APIs such as Speech (speech to text), Language (PII detection) and Vision (OCR). It's a family of capabilities, not the platform.\n- **Azure AI Services** is the *old* name for those tools, not for the studio.\n- **Foundry IQ** is Azure AI Search used as an agent's knowledge source. For example, an agent grounded on a knowledge base of your product manuals.\n\n**Source:** [what is foundry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Microsoft Foundry"},{"key":"B","text":"Foundry Tools"},{"key":"C","text":"Azure AI Services"},{"key":"D","text":"Foundry IQ"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.1-2","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.1 · What Microsoft Foundry is","scenario":"","stem":"Match each older Azure AI concept to its current equivalent in Microsoft Foundry.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Foundry consolidated several services. Agents moved from the Assistants API to the **Responses API**, versioning moved to the **v1** routes, the hub-plus-resources model became **one Foundry resource with projects**, and threads/messages/runs became **conversations, items and responses**.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Assistants → Responses API:** instead of `threads.create`, `messages.create` and `runs.create`, one call: `client.responses.create(model=\"chat\", input=\"Summarize this ticket\", previous_response_id=prev.id)`.\n- **Monthly `api-version` → v1:** `https://contoso.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses`, with no `?api-version=2024-10-21` to keep bumping, and it works with the standard OpenAI SDK.\n- **Hub + resources → one Foundry resource:** one `AIServices` resource holds the model deployments, and projects inside it group each team's agents and evaluations.\n- **Threads, Messages, Runs → Conversations, Items, Responses:** a conversation stores the history, items are the messages and tool calls in it, and each response is one model turn.\n- **Azure Machine Learning workspace** is what classic *hubs* were built on. It didn't become any of these. **Prompt flow** is a classic-portal tool for chaining LLM calls, not the new name of any of them.\n\n**Source:** [what is foundry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Assistants API (Agents v0.5/v1)","Monthly `api-version` parameters","Hub + Azure OpenAI + Azure AI Services resources","Threads, Messages, Runs"],"values":["A single Foundry resource with projects","Azure Machine Learning workspace","Conversations, Items, Responses","Prompt flow","Responses API (Agents v2)","v1 stable routes (`/openai/v1/`)"],"answer":["Responses API (Agents v2)","v1 stable routes (`/openai/v1/`)","A single Foundry resource with projects","Conversations, Items, Responses"]}},{"id":"aoai-1.1-3","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.1 · What Microsoft Foundry is","scenario":"","stem":"You need an agent that uses a model, instructions and a few built-in tools. You don't want to write or host any application code or containers. Which kind of Foundry agent fits?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"A **prompt agent** is declarative: you choose a model, write instructions and attach tools in the portal or SDK, and Foundry hosts and runs it. A **hosted agent** is the full-code option: you package your own code or framework (Agent Framework, LangGraph, Semantic Kernel) as a container and Foundry runs it with a managed endpoint.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Prompt agent:** in the portal you pick `gpt-4.1`, write *\"You answer HR policy questions. Cite the policy section.\"*, add the File Search tool with the HR handbook, and Foundry hosts it. Nothing to deploy.\n- **Hosted agent:** you write the agent in code (for example with LangGraph or Agent Framework), build a container image, and Foundry runs it behind a managed endpoint. That's code and a container, which the requirement rules out.\n- **A container app with Semantic Kernel:** you'd write the app *and* host it in Azure Container Apps yourself.\n- **An Azure Function with an OpenAI binding:** still application code that you write, deploy and run in a Function App.\n\n**Source:** [what is foundry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A prompt agent"},{"key":"B","text":"A hosted agent"},{"key":"C","text":"A container app with Semantic Kernel"},{"key":"D","text":"An Azure Function with an OpenAI binding"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.2-1","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.2 · Resource types, projects and connected resources","scenario":"","stem":"You create a Foundry resource with the Azure CLI. Which resource type and kind does it use?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"A Foundry resource is `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` of kind **`AIServices`**, and a Foundry project is the sub-resource `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects`. A standalone Azure OpenAI resource uses the same provider with kind `OpenAI`. Because they share the `Microsoft.CognitiveServices` namespace, existing Azure Policy definitions and RBAC actions keep working after you upgrade from Azure OpenAI to Foundry.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`AIServices`:**\n\n  ```bash\n  az cognitiveservices account create -n contoso-foundry -g rg -l eastus \\\n    --kind AIServices --sku S0 --custom-domain contoso-foundry\n  ```\n\n  Projects are then sub-resources of type `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects`.\n- **Kind `OpenAI`** is a standalone Azure OpenAI resource: model deployments only, without Foundry projects or agents.\n- **`MachineLearningServices/workspaces` with kind `Hub`** is the *classic* hub-based setup, which Foundry resources replace.\n- **`Microsoft.Search/searchServices`** is Azure AI Search, a separate service that you *connect* to Foundry (as Foundry IQ). It has no `Foundry` kind.\n\n**Source:** [architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` with kind `AIServices`"},{"key":"B","text":"`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` with kind `OpenAI`"},{"key":"C","text":"`Microsoft.MachineLearningServices/workspaces` with kind `Hub`"},{"key":"D","text":"`Microsoft.Search/searchServices` with kind `Foundry`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.2-2","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.2 · Resource types, projects and connected resources","scenario":"","stem":"A Foundry resource has two projects, a connected Azure Storage account and a connected Azure Key Vault. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Connected resources (Storage, Key Vault, Foundry IQ) are **independent Azure resources** with their own networking, access policies and compliance settings. The Foundry resource holds governance settings and model deployments; projects are development boundaries that reuse those deployments and connections. RBAC can be scoped at both levels. Most new APIs work at project scope, but some older account-level APIs, such as Translator, are only on the resource.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Network rules don't carry over** to the connected resources. If you disable public access on the Foundry resource but not on its connected Storage account, the storage account is still reachable from the internet. Lock it down with its own firewall or private endpoint.\n- **Projects reuse deployments:** deploy `gpt-4o` once as `chat` on the resource, and agents in both the `hr-bot` and `it-bot` projects can call `chat`.\n- **RBAC at both scopes:** assigning **Azure AI User** on just the `hr-bot` project lets the HR developers build there without seeing `it-bot`.\n- **Resource-only capabilities:** for example, Translator's document translation is called on the resource endpoint, not through a project.\n\n**Source:** [architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Network rules you set on the Foundry resource also apply to the connected Storage account.","Projects can reuse the model deployments of their Foundry resource.","Role assignments can be scoped to the Foundry resource or to a single project.","Some capabilities, such as the Translator API, are available only at the Foundry resource level, not at the project scope."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-1.2-3","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.2 · Resource types, projects and connected resources","scenario":"","stem":"Your workload only calls an Azure OpenAI chat model. It doesn't need agents or evaluations. What do the Foundry architecture docs say about the resource to use?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"For most scenarios (agents, deployments, evaluations) a **Foundry resource** is the recommended starting point, and a Foundry resource with one project is the default for a single developer. If the workload only needs Azure OpenAI completions, a **standalone Azure OpenAI resource** might be sufficient. Hub-based projects belong to the classic portal.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **A standalone Azure OpenAI resource might be enough:** a chatbot that only calls `chat.completions` doesn't use agents, evaluations or projects, so a `kind OpenAI` resource does the job.\n- **A hub-based project first** is false. Hubs belong to the classic portal, and new work starts with a Foundry resource (or here, maybe just Azure OpenAI).\n- **Only into a project** is false. Models are deployed on the *resource*, and projects reuse those deployments.\n- **A resource per region of use** is false. Users anywhere can call one endpoint, and a Global Standard deployment already routes the processing across regions.\n\n**Source:** [architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A standalone Azure OpenAI resource might be enough; a Foundry resource with one project is the default for most other scenarios"},{"key":"B","text":"You must create a hub-based project first"},{"key":"C","text":"Azure OpenAI models can only be deployed into a Foundry project, never into a resource"},{"key":"D","text":"You need a Foundry resource in each region the app is used from"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.3-1","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.3 · Creating a resource and deploying a model","scenario":"","stem":"Your code calls a model you deployed with `--model-name gpt-4o --deployment-name MyModel`. What must the code pass to identify the model?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Azure OpenAI always routes by **deployment name**, even in the `model` parameter of the request. This is one of the key differences from OpenAI, which only needs the model name. Choose deployment names carefully, because they're what your code refers to.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`MyModel`:**\n\n  ```python\n  client = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://myres.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/\", api_key=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"])\n  client.chat.completions.create(model=\"MyModel\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hi\"}])\n  ```\n\n- **`gpt-4o`** works with OpenAI's own API, but on Azure it returns **404 `DeploymentNotFound`** unless you happen to have a deployment *named* `gpt-4o`. Many teams name deployments after the model to avoid confusion.\n- **The model version** is chosen when you deploy, and the request never names it.\n- **The resource name** is already in the endpoint URL (`myres.openai.azure.com`). It doesn't identify a model.\n\n**Source:** [create resource](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/create-resource)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`MyModel`, the deployment name"},{"key":"B","text":"`gpt-4o`, the model name"},{"key":"C","text":"The model version, `2024-11-20`"},{"key":"D","text":"The resource name"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.3-2","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.3 · Creating a resource and deploying a model","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the Azure CLI command that creates an Azure OpenAI resource with a custom subdomain.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"An Azure OpenAI resource is a Cognitive Services account of kind **`OpenAI`** (a Foundry resource is kind `AIServices`). Azure OpenAI only offers the **Standard** pricing tier, `s0`. `GlobalStandard` and `Standard` are deployment SKUs used later with `az cognitiveservices account deployment create --sku-name`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`AIServices`** creates a **Foundry** resource, which can also host Azure OpenAI models, but the question asks for an Azure OpenAI resource.\n- **`F0`** is the free tier that some Cognitive Services offer. Azure OpenAI has none, so the command fails.\n- **`GlobalStandard`** and **`Standard`** are *deployment* SKUs, used later: `az cognitiveservices account deployment create ... --sku-name GlobalStandard`. The resource itself is always `s0`.\n\n`--custom-domain MyOpenAIResource` gives the endpoint `https://myopenairesource.openai.azure.com/`. You need a custom subdomain for Microsoft Entra ID authentication and private endpoints.\n\n**Source:** [create resource](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/create-resource)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"az cognitiveservices account create \\\n  --name MyOpenAIResource \\\n  --resource-group OAIResourceGroup \\\n  --location eastus \\\n  --kind [[0]] \\\n  --sku [[1]] \\\n  --custom-domain MyOpenAIResource","values":["AIServices","F0","GlobalStandard","OpenAI","Standard","s0"],"answer":["OpenAI","s0"]}},{"id":"aoai-1.3-3","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.3 · Creating a resource and deploying a model","scenario":"","stem":"When you create an Azure OpenAI resource in the Azure portal, you choose **Disabled** on the **Network** tab. What happens?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The three options are **All networks** (the default), **Selected networks** (chosen virtual networks and subnets, plus optional IP ranges), and **Disabled**. With Disabled, nothing can reach the resource until you add private endpoints, which become the exclusive access path.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Disabled:** a call from your laptop or any VNet fails with 403 until you add a private endpoint (group ID `account`, private DNS zone `privatelink.openai.azure.com`). Then only clients that resolve the name through that endpoint get in.\n- **Only Azure services in the same region:** no such option exists. The closest is **Selected networks** with a trusted-services exception.\n- **Key authentication disabled** is a different setting, `disableLocalAuth: true`. It controls *how* callers authenticate, not *which networks* can reach the resource.\n- **Deployments stopped** is false. Networking never stops deployments; it only filters who can reach them.\n\n**Source:** [create resource](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/create-resource)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"No network can reach the resource; private endpoint connections become the only way in"},{"key":"B","text":"Only Azure services in the same region can reach it"},{"key":"C","text":"Key authentication is disabled and only Microsoft Entra ID works"},{"key":"D","text":"The resource is created but its deployments are stopped"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.3-4","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.3 · Creating a resource and deploying a model","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the Azure CLI command that deploys `gpt-4o` as a Global Standard deployment named `chat`.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`--deployment-name` is the name your code calls; `--model-name` and `--model-version` pick the model; `--model-format OpenAI`; and `--sku-name` is the deployment type: `Standard`, `GlobalStandard`, `GlobalBatch` or `ProvisionedManaged` (no hyphens in the CLI). `--sku-capacity` is in units of the deployment type (thousands of tokens per minute for Standard types).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`AIServices`** is a resource *kind*, not a model format. The model format for OpenAI models is `OpenAI`, and models from other providers use that provider's format.\n- **`Global-Standard`** is how the portal displays the type. The CLI rejects the hyphen and only accepts `GlobalStandard`.\n- **`ProvisionedManaged`** is **Regional Provisioned**: reserved PTUs in the resource's region, billed per hour. Here, `--sku-capacity 1` would mean 1 PTU, which is below any model's minimum.\n\n**Source:** [create resource](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/create-resource)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"az cognitiveservices account deployment create \\\n  --name MyOpenAIResource \\\n  --resource-group OAIResourceGroup \\\n  --deployment-name [[0]] \\\n  --model-name [[1]] \\\n  --model-version \"2024-11-20\" \\\n  --model-format [[2]] \\\n  --sku-capacity 1 \\\n  --sku-name [[3]]","values":["AIServices","Global-Standard","GlobalStandard","OpenAI","ProvisionedManaged","chat","gpt-4o"],"answer":["chat","gpt-4o","OpenAI","GlobalStandard"]}},{"id":"aoai-1.3-5","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.3 · Creating a resource and deploying a model","scenario":"","stem":"Which Azure CLI command returns the REST endpoint of an Azure OpenAI resource?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`az cognitiveservices account show` returns the resource, and the endpoint is `properties.endpoint`. `az cognitiveservices account keys list` returns `key1` and `key2`. Deleting a deployment is `az cognitiveservices account deployment delete`, and deleting the resource is `az cognitiveservices account delete`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`account show`:** `az cognitiveservices account show -n MyOpenAIResource -g OAIResourceGroup --query properties.endpoint -o tsv` prints `https://myopenairesource.openai.azure.com/`.\n- **`keys list`** returns only `{ \"key1\": \"...\", \"key2\": \"...\" }`. There's no endpoint in it.\n- **`az openai endpoint show`** doesn't exist. Azure OpenAI is managed through `az cognitiveservices`.\n- **`az resource list --resource-type OpenAI`:** the resource type is `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts` (OpenAI is the *kind*), and generic resource objects have no `.uri`.\n\n**Source:** [create resource](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/create-resource)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`az cognitiveservices account show --name <res> --resource-group <rg>` and read `.properties.endpoint`"},{"key":"B","text":"`az cognitiveservices account keys list` and read `.endpoint`"},{"key":"C","text":"`az openai endpoint show`"},{"key":"D","text":"`az resource list --resource-type OpenAI` and read `.uri`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.4-1","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.4 · Data storage, keys and regions","scenario":"","stem":"You want to encrypt a Foundry resource's data at rest with your own key. Which prerequisites must be met? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"By default data at rest is encrypted with Microsoft-managed keys (FIPS 140-2 compliant 256-bit AES), with no code changes. For **customer-managed keys**: the Key Vault must be in the **same region**, have **soft delete and purge protection** on, and the managed identity needs key permissions such as **Key Vault Crypto User** (with Azure RBAC).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Same region:** a Foundry resource in East US can't use a key in a West Europe vault. Create the vault in East US.\n- **Soft delete and purge protection:** `az keyvault update -n kv-ai --enable-purge-protection true`. That stops someone from purging the key and making the resource's data unreadable for good.\n- **Key permissions:** give the Foundry resource's managed identity **Key Vault Crypto User** on the vault (or wrap, unwrap and get in an access policy).\n- **Premium with an HSM-backed key** is false. A Standard vault with a software-protected RSA key is enough.\n- **Public access disabled** is false. Encryption at rest and network access are independent settings.\n\n**Source:** [architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The Key Vault is in the same Azure region as the Foundry resource"},{"key":"B","text":"Soft delete and purge protection are enabled on the Key Vault"},{"key":"C","text":"The managed identity has key permissions, such as the Key Vault Crypto User role"},{"key":"D","text":"The Key Vault uses the Premium SKU with an HSM-backed key"},{"key":"E","text":"Public network access to the Foundry resource is disabled"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-1.4-2","domain":3,"section":"1","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 1.4 · Data storage, keys and regions","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Foundry data and regions.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Foundry has **no automatic cross-region failover**; for multi-region availability you deploy separate Foundry resources and route at the application layer. Connection secrets go to a managed Key Vault unless you connect your own, and one Key Vault connection covers all project and resource-level secrets. Data at rest stays in the resource's geography, but inference follows the deployment type: global can use any region, data zone stays in the US or EU zone, and standard/regional stays in the deployment's region.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **No automatic failover:** if East US has an outage, a resource there is unavailable. For resilience, create a second Foundry resource in, say, West US with the same deployments, and have the app (or API Management in front of it) switch endpoints.\n- **Managed Key Vault:** when you add a connection with an API key (say, to an external search service), the key is stored in a Key Vault that Foundry manages for you, unless you connect your own.\n- **One vault for all projects:** with your own Key Vault connected, the `hr-bot` and `it-bot` projects' secrets both go into it.\n- **Global Standard can process anywhere:** a prompt sent to a Global Standard deployment of an East US resource might be processed in Sweden, while stored data (files, conversations) stays in the US.\n\n**Source:** [architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/architecture)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Foundry automatically fails over to another region if its region goes down.","By default, API-key connection secrets are stored in a managed Azure Key Vault.","One Azure Key Vault connection holds the connection secrets for the resource and all its projects.","A Global Standard deployment might process a prompt in any Azure region."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.1-1","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.1 · The model catalog: sold by Azure and from partners","scenario":"","stem":"The Foundry model catalog has two main categories: **Foundry Models sold by Azure** and **Foundry Models from partners and community**. Answer Yes or No for each statement about models sold by Azure.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Models **sold by Azure** are hosted and operated by Azure as part of the Foundry Models service. They include **all Azure OpenAI models** plus selected models from other providers (for example Cohere, DeepSeek, Meta, Mistral AI, Microsoft and xAI). They're billed through your subscription, covered by Azure SLAs and supported by Microsoft. Models from partners and community are a separate category.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **All Azure OpenAI models:** for example, `gpt-4.1`, `o4-mini` and `text-embedding-3-large`, deployed and billed like any Azure service.\n- **Billed through Azure, with SLAs:** deploying `DeepSeek-R1` or `Mistral-Large` from the *sold by Azure* list appears on your Azure bill with Azure's SLA, without a separate contract with the provider.\n- **Supported by Microsoft:** you open an Azure support ticket for them, as for any Azure service.\n- **Only OpenAI models** is false. Sold-by-Azure models also come from Cohere, DeepSeek, Meta, Mistral AI, Microsoft and xAI. Partner and community models are the other category, with the provider's own terms.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["All Azure OpenAI models are sold by Azure.","They're billed through your Azure subscription and covered by Azure service-level agreements.","They're supported by Microsoft.","Only OpenAI models can be sold by Azure."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.1-2","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.1 · The model catalog: sold by Azure and from partners","scenario":"","stem":"You need a model for multi-step math and coding problems, and you accept that it spends extra time processing the request before answering. Which model family is designed for this?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The **o-series** are reasoning models: they spend more time processing and understanding the request, which makes them strong at science, coding and math. Embedding models only turn text into vectors, `-tts` models are text to speech, and Sora generates video. Newer GPT-5 and later models also reason.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **o-series:** given *\"Prove that the sum of two odd numbers is even, then write a unit test for a function that checks it\"*, `o4-mini` reasons through the steps (using reasoning tokens you're billed for) before it answers.\n- **`text-embedding-3-large`** turns text into a vector of numbers for search and similarity. It can't answer a question at all.\n- **`gpt-4o-mini-tts`** reads text aloud, returning audio.\n- **`sora-2`** generates video from a prompt.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The o-series reasoning models, such as `o3` and `o4-mini`"},{"key":"B","text":"`text-embedding-3-large`"},{"key":"C","text":"`gpt-4o-mini-tts`"},{"key":"D","text":"`sora-2`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.1-3","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.1 · The model catalog: sold by Azure and from partners","scenario":"","stem":"You want to deploy `gpt-oss-120b` like your other Azure OpenAI models. What's different about it?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`gpt-oss-120b` and `gpt-oss-20b` are open-weight reasoning models (text in, text out, Chat Completions, function calling, structured outputs). Unlike other Azure OpenAI models, `gpt-oss-120b` **requires a Foundry project** to deploy. `gpt-oss-20b` is available through managed compute and Foundry Local.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Needs a Foundry project:** you deploy it from a Foundry project in the portal, not on a standalone Azure OpenAI resource the way you would `gpt-4o`.\n- **Only Provisioned** is false. It isn't limited to provisioned deployments.\n- **Only the Responses API** is false. It supports Chat Completions, with function calling and structured outputs.\n- **An embedding model** is false. It's a text-in, text-out reasoning model. Its smaller sibling, `gpt-oss-20b`, can even run on your laptop with Foundry Local.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It needs a Foundry project to deploy"},{"key":"B","text":"It can only be deployed as Provisioned"},{"key":"C","text":"It only supports the Responses API"},{"key":"D","text":"It's an embedding model"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.2-1","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.2 · Context windows and token limits","scenario":"","stem":"A model's capabilities table lists a context window, plus separate input and output token limits. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The input and output values are **individual limits**, not additive allowances. Input, output and reasoning tokens all share the context budget, so a longer prompt leaves less room for generation. A parameter like `max_output_tokens` only caps the output; it doesn't reserve tokens.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nTake a model with a 128,000-token context window and a 16,384-token output limit.\n\n- **Shared budget:** a 120,000-token prompt leaves at most 8,000 tokens for reasoning and output, even though the output limit says 16,384.\n- **An upper limit:** `max_output_tokens: 4000` caps the answer, but a short question still gets a short answer.\n- **The limits add up** is false. 128,000 input plus 16,384 output isn't available in one request.\n- **Reserving tokens** is false. With a 125,000-token prompt, `max_output_tokens: 10000` doesn't make room. The model stops when the context is full.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Input tokens, generated output tokens and reasoning tokens share the available context budget"},{"key":"B","text":"The max output tokens value is an upper limit, not a guaranteed output size"},{"key":"C","text":"The input and output limits always add up, so you can use both in full in the same request"},{"key":"D","text":"Setting `max_output_tokens` reserves that many tokens for the answer even if the prompt is long"}],"answer":["A","B"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.2-2","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.2 · Context windows and token limits","scenario":"","stem":"For pricing, a model has short-context and long-context categories. What decides which category a request falls in?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"*Context* here means the **input tokens of the individual request**. It's not the model's maximum context window, and generated output tokens don't change the category (they're still billed at the model's output rate). A large-context model can still receive a short-context request.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Input tokens of the request:** with a model whose long-context price starts above a threshold, a request with a 10,000-token prompt is billed at the short-context rate, and one with a prompt over the threshold at the long-context rate, on the same deployment.\n- **The model's maximum context window** is fixed for the model, so it can't decide per request.\n- **Input plus output** is false. Output tokens are billed at the output rate but don't move the request into the long-context category.\n- **Output tokens only** is false for the same reason: the category depends only on the prompt.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The number of input tokens in that request"},{"key":"B","text":"The model's maximum context window"},{"key":"C","text":"The combined number of input and output tokens"},{"key":"D","text":"The number of output tokens generated"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.3-1","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.3 · Embedding models","scenario":"","stem":"Match each embedding model to its default number of output dimensions.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`text-embedding-3-large` returns **3,072** dimensions; `text-embedding-3-small` and `text-embedding-ada-002` return **1,536**. All three accept up to **8,192** tokens per input (ada-002 version 1 accepted only 2,046). The third-generation models also accept a `dimensions` parameter to return shorter vectors.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **3,072:** `text-embedding-3-large`. With `dimensions=1024`, you get 1,024 numbers instead, for a smaller index.\n- **1,536:** `text-embedding-3-small` and `text-embedding-ada-002`. A vector column for them is declared `VECTOR(1536)` in SQL.\n- **4,096** isn't a default for any of the three.\n- **768** is a common size for smaller open models. You can *ask* a third-generation model for 768 with `dimensions=768`, but it's never the default.\n- **8,192** is the maximum number of *tokens* per input, not a vector size.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`text-embedding-3-large`","`text-embedding-3-small`","`text-embedding-ada-002`"],"values":["1,536","3,072","4,096","768","8,192"],"answer":["3,072","1,536","1,536"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.3-2","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.3 · Embedding models","scenario":"","stem":"Your vector store holds embeddings from `text-embedding-ada-002`. You want to use `text-embedding-3-large`. What do you have to do?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"You **can't upgrade between embedding models**: vectors from different models aren't comparable, even when they have the same length. To move from `text-embedding-ada-002` to `text-embedding-3-large`, re-embed your content (and your queries) with the new model.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Re-embed everything:** run all your documents (and, from now on, every query) through the new deployment, store the new vectors, and swap indexes when it's done.\n- **The upgrade policy converts vectors** is false. `versionUpgradeOption` moves a deployment to a newer *version of the same model*. It never touches vectors you've already stored.\n- **Padding with zeros to 3,072** gives vectors of the right length, but they're still ada-002's vectors. Similarity scores between them and new-model query vectors are meaningless.\n- **`dimensions: 1536`** makes the new model return 1,536 numbers, but they're in a different space from ada-002's, so mixing them still gives wrong search results.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Generate new embeddings for all the content with the new model"},{"key":"B","text":"Nothing: set the deployment's upgrade policy and the stored vectors are converted"},{"key":"C","text":"Pad the stored 1,536-dimension vectors with zeros to 3,072"},{"key":"D","text":"Call the embeddings API with `dimensions: 1536` and keep the old vectors"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.3-3","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.3 · Embedding models","scenario":"","stem":"Which statements about the third-generation embedding models (`text-embedding-3-small` and `-large`) are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The `dimensions` parameter trades a little accuracy for less compute, memory and storage. OpenAI's MTEB testing found the new models still beat ada-002 when reduced below 1,536 dimensions. An array input can hold up to **2,048** items per call. Embedding models work only with **Embeddings API** requests. 8,192 is the token limit per input, not the dimension count.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`dimensions`:**\n\n  ```python\n  r = client.embeddings.create(model=\"emb-large\", input=[\"refund policy\", \"shipping times\"], dimensions=1024)\n  len(r.data[0].embedding)   # 1024\n  ```\n\n- **Still better than ada-002** below 1,536 dimensions, on OpenAI's MTEB benchmark. So shrinking vectors to save storage doesn't cost you the upgrade.\n- **2,048 inputs per call:** the `input` list above could hold up to 2,048 strings, so you embed a batch in one request instead of 2,048.\n- **Chat Completions** is false. `client.chat.completions.create(model=\"emb-large\", ...)` fails, because embedding deployments only accept `/embeddings`.\n- **8,192 dimensions** is false. 8,192 is the token limit per input. The dimensions are 1,536 (small) or 3,072 (large) by default.\n\n**Source:** [models sold directly by azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They support a `dimensions` parameter that returns a shorter embedding"},{"key":"B","text":"Even reduced below 1,536 dimensions, they performed slightly better than `text-embedding-ada-002` on OpenAI's MTEB tests"},{"key":"C","text":"One request can embed an array of up to 2,048 inputs"},{"key":"D","text":"They can be called through the Chat Completions API"},{"key":"E","text":"They return 8,192 dimensions"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.4-1","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.4 · Model versions and upgrade policies","scenario":"","stem":"Match each `versionUpgradeOption` value to what it does.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"These are the three upgrade policies (in the portal: **Upgrade once new default version becomes available**, **Once the current version expires**, and **Opt out of automatic model version upgrades**). A `null` value means `OnceCurrentVersionExpired`. Automatic updates apply only to **Standard** deployment types; provisioned deployments are migrated manually.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`OnceNewDefaultVersionAvailable`:** when `2024-11-20` becomes `gpt-4o`'s default, the deployment moves to it within two weeks, whether or not the old version is retiring.\n- **`OnceCurrentVersionExpired`:** the deployment stays on `2024-08-06` until that version's retirement date, then moves to the default.\n- **`NoAutoUpgrade`:** the deployment stays put. On the retirement date, its calls start failing until you upgrade it yourself.\n- **Upgrading to the newest preview** isn't an option. Automatic upgrades only ever go to the *default* (GA) version, never to a preview.\n\nYou set it in the REST body, for example `\"properties\": { \"versionUpgradeOption\": \"NoAutoUpgrade\", \"model\": { ... } }`.\n\n**Source:** [working with models](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/working-with-models) · [model versions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/model-versions)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`OnceNewDefaultVersionAvailable`","`OnceCurrentVersionExpired`","`NoAutoUpgrade`"],"values":["Never upgrades; the deployment stops working when the version is retired","Upgrades to the current default version when the deployed version reaches its retirement date","Upgrades to the new default version within two weeks of it becoming the default","Upgrades to the newest preview version as soon as it's released"],"answer":["Upgrades to the new default version within two weeks of it becoming the default","Upgrades to the current default version when the deployed version reaches its retirement date","Never upgrades; the deployment stops working when the version is retired"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.4-2","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.4 · Model versions and upgrade policies","scenario":"","stem":"A deployment's properties don't show a `versionUpgradeOption`. How does it behave when its model version is retired?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"A missing (`null`) `versionUpgradeOption` is equivalent to **`OnceCurrentVersionExpired`**. Once you set the value explicitly, it shows in the portal and REST API. You can read the option with REST, PowerShell or the Azure CLI, but the CLI can't update it; use REST (`PUT …/deployments/{name}`) or PowerShell.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Upgrades at retirement:** a deployment created years ago with no option set keeps working past its version's retirement date, because it moves to the default version then.\n- **`null` means `NoAutoUpgrade`** is false. If it did, old deployments would break when their versions retired.\n- **Upgrades within two weeks of any new default** is `OnceNewDefaultVersionAvailable`, which has to be set explicitly.\n- **It's deleted** is false. Retirement never deletes a deployment. Even a `NoAutoUpgrade` deployment stays, it just fails until you upgrade it.\n\n**Source:** [working with models](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/working-with-models) · [model versions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/model-versions)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It upgrades to the default version, because `null` means `OnceCurrentVersionExpired`"},{"key":"B","text":"It stops working, because `null` means `NoAutoUpgrade`"},{"key":"C","text":"It upgrades within two weeks of any new default version"},{"key":"D","text":"It's deleted"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.4-3","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.4 · Model versions and upgrade policies","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about model and API versions.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"There are two versions: the **model version** (for example `gpt-4o` `2024-11-20`) and the **API version** your code uses. An updated set of model weights doesn't necessarily need a new API version. Azure gives at least two weeks' notice before changing the default version, and during the upgrade window it deploys the new version to a region to perform the upgrade if necessary.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Two different versions:** a call to `?api-version=2024-10-21` can hit a deployment running `gpt-4o` `2024-08-06` or `2024-11-20`. The API version decides the request and response shape, and the model version decides the weights.\n- **A new model version always needs a new API version** is false. When `gpt-4o` `2024-11-20` came out, existing code kept working on the same API version.\n- **Two weeks' notice:** before `2024-11-20` becomes the default, subscriptions get at least two weeks' notice, which is time to test it in a second deployment.\n- **Upgrading where the version isn't listed yet:** Azure can deploy the new version to your region just to upgrade your deployment during the window, even if the portal doesn't list it there yet.\n\n**Source:** [working with models](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/working-with-models) · [model versions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/model-versions)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The `api-version` query parameter is the contract your code uses, separate from the model version.","A new model version always requires a new API version.","Azure notifies customers at least two weeks before a new version becomes a model's default version.","Azure can upgrade a deployment during the scheduled window even if the new version isn't yet listed as available in that region."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.5-1","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.5 · Lifecycle stages, retirement and notifications","scenario":"","stem":"Match each model lifecycle stage to its description.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The five stages are Preview, Generally Available (production-ready, the unlabelled default), **Legacy** (optional, migrate soon), **Deprecated** (existing customers only; \"existing\" is decided per **subscription**, and a new subscription in the same tenant doesn't inherit access), and **Retired** (`410 Gone`).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Preview:** for example, a `-preview` model version whose behaviour or request schema can still change. Fine for trying out, not for production.\n- **Legacy:** a newer model exists (say, `gpt-4.1` alongside `gpt-4o`). Nothing breaks yet, but it's time to plan the move.\n- **Deprecated:** a subscription that already has a deployment of the version can create more, but a new subscription (even in the same tenant) can't deploy it.\n- **Retired:** every call returns **410 Gone**.\n- **Production-ready with fixed weights and APIs** is the **Generally Available** stage, between Preview and Legacy. It's the one stage not in this list.\n\n**Source:** [model retirements](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirements)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Preview","Legacy","Deprecated","Retired"],"values":["All inference requests return `410 Gone`","Experimental; weights and API schema might change","Newer models exist; plan to migrate (an optional stage)","Only subscriptions that already deployed the version can create deployments","Production-ready with fixed weights and APIs"],"answer":["Experimental; weights and API schema might change","Newer models exist; plan to migrate (an optional stage)","Only subscriptions that already deployed the version can create deployments","All inference requests return `410 Gone`"]}},{"id":"aoai-2.5-2","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.5 · Lifecycle stages, retirement and notifications","scenario":"","stem":"A GA Azure OpenAI model follows the standard lifecycle. When is it deprecated for new customers, and when is it retired?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The standard GA lifecycle is **18 months**: the retirement date is set programmatically at launch and readable from the Models API. At **12 months** the model is deprecated (existing customers only). The replacement is usually testable in Global Standard about 90 days before retirement. Models from some publishers, such as Anthropic, DeepSeek and Mistral AI, follow a 12-month lifecycle instead.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **12 and 18 months:** a model launched in January 2026 is deprecated for new customers in January 2027 and retired in July 2027. The retirement date is known from launch day, so you can read it from the Models API and plan.\n- **6 and 12 months** is too short for Azure OpenAI's standard lifecycle. A 12-month lifecycle does apply to models from some other publishers, such as DeepSeek and Mistral AI.\n- **18 and 24 months** is off by six months on both dates.\n- **Never retired while in use** is false. Retirement happens on the date whether or not deployments still use the model. They're auto-upgraded (Standard types) or start failing.\n\n**Source:** [model retirements](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirements)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Deprecated at 12 months, retired at 18 months after launch"},{"key":"B","text":"Deprecated at 6 months, retired at 12 months"},{"key":"C","text":"Deprecated at 18 months, retired at 24 months"},{"key":"D","text":"It's never retired while any deployment uses it"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.5-3","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.5 · Lifecycle stages, retirement and notifications","scenario":"","stem":"A model version is retiring. Which deployments does Microsoft upgrade automatically to the replacement? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Microsoft auto-upgrades **Global Standard, Data Zone Standard and Standard** deployments, on a rolling, region-by-region schedule published in advance. **Provisioned deployments are never auto-upgraded**: create a replacement deployment and move traffic before the retirement date. The shared retirement date doesn't imply automatic upgrades for Global Batch either.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Global Standard, Data Zone Standard and Standard** move to the replacement on Microsoft's published rolling schedule, region by region.\n- **Global Provisioned** and **Data Zone Provisioned** are never upgraded for you. Create a provisioned deployment of the new model next to the old one, move traffic over, test, and delete the old deployment before the retirement date. Otherwise it starts failing.\n\n**Source:** [model retirements](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirements)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Global Standard"},{"key":"B","text":"Data Zone Standard"},{"key":"C","text":"Standard"},{"key":"D","text":"Global Provisioned"},{"key":"E","text":"Data Zone Provisioned"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-2.5-4","domain":3,"section":"2","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 2.5 · Lifecycle stages, retirement and notifications","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about model launches and notifications.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Models launch in **Global Standard** first, then Global Provisioned, then Data Zone, and regional Standard/Provisioned last. GA retirement notices go out at least **60 days** ahead; preview notices at least **30 days**. When a preview is retired, deployments are force-upgraded to a newer preview or the GA model (or the model retires if there's no replacement); there's no option to stay. Previews aren't recommended for production.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Global Standard first:** a new model such as `gpt-5` can be called on a Global Standard deployment weeks before you can deploy it as Data Zone Standard in an EU region.\n- **60 days' notice:** subscription owners with active deployments get the GA retirement email at least 60 days ahead (30 days for previews).\n- **Staying on a retired preview** is false. When Microsoft retires a preview, the deployment is force-upgraded to a newer preview or the GA version, or retired if there's no replacement.\n- **Previews for production** is false. Previews can change weights and schema with short notice, so run production on GA versions.\n\n**Source:** [model retirements](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirements)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Every new model launches first in Global Standard.","Subscription owners with active deployments get a retirement notice at least 60 days before a GA model retires.","You can stay on a preview model version after Microsoft decides to retire it.","Preview models are recommended for production workloads."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.1-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.1 · Deployment types and where data is processed","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the deployment type the docs recommend.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Global Standard** is the default starting point. **Data Zone** types keep processing in the US, EU or APAC zone. **Provisioned** types reserve capacity (PTUs). **Batch** types process large asynchronous jobs at a 50% discount. **Developer** is only for evaluating fine-tuned models: 24-hour lifetime, no SLA and no data-residency guarantee.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Global Standard:** pay per token, routed to wherever there's capacity worldwide. Usually the first type a new model is available in.\n- **Data Zone Standard:** for a European company with a policy that EU prompts stay in the EU. Processing stays in EU regions, still pay per token.\n- **Global Provisioned:** for example, 100 PTUs reserved for a call-center assistant with steady traffic, with no requirement about which region processes it.\n- **Global Batch:** submit a `.jsonl` of a million product descriptions to classify overnight, at half the price.\n- **Developer:** deploy a fine-tuned model for 24 hours to try it, without paying for hosting a full deployment.\n- **Regional Provisioned** would also reserve throughput, but it pins processing to the resource's region, a restriction this requirement doesn't need, and new models usually reach it last.\n\n**Source:** [deployment types](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Newest models, lowest price, broadest regions","Prompts must only be processed inside the EU data zone, pay per token","Reserved, predictable throughput with no routing restriction","Millions of non-urgent requests at lower cost","Temporarily evaluate a fine-tuned model"],"values":["Data Zone Standard","Developer","Global Batch","Global Provisioned","Global Standard","Regional Provisioned"],"answer":["Global Standard","Data Zone Standard","Global Provisioned","Global Batch","Developer"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.1-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.1 · Deployment types and where data is processed","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the table of deployment SKU codes used in code and the CLI.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The SKU codes are `GlobalStandard`, `GlobalProvisionedManaged`, `GlobalBatch`, `DataZoneStandard`, `DataZoneProvisionedManaged`, `DataZoneBatch`, `Standard`, `ProvisionedManaged` (Regional Provisioned) and `DeveloperTier`. The portal shows display names such as `Global-Batch`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`Global-Standard`** is the portal's display name, and the CLI rejects it. **`Standard`** is *regional* Standard, a different type.\n- **`DataZone`** and **`ZoneStandard`** don't exist.\n- **`Provisioned`** and **`RegionalProvisioned`** don't exist. Regional Provisioned's code is the older-looking `ProvisionedManaged`.\n- **`GlobalProvisioned`** and **`ProvisionedGlobal`** don't exist. The code ends in `Managed`, as `GlobalProvisionedManaged`.\n- **`BatchDataZone`** doesn't exist, and **`GlobalBatch`** is the *global* batch type, which isn't limited to a data zone.\n\nFor example: `az cognitiveservices account deployment create ... --sku-name DataZoneStandard --sku-capacity 50`.\n\n**Source:** [deployment types](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"Global Standard        -> [[0]]\nData Zone Standard     -> [[1]]\nRegional Provisioned   -> [[2]]\nGlobal Provisioned     -> [[3]]\nData Zone Batch        -> [[4]]","options":[["Global-Standard","GlobalStandard","Standard"],["DataZone","DataZoneStandard","ZoneStandard"],["Provisioned","ProvisionedManaged","RegionalProvisioned"],["GlobalProvisioned","GlobalProvisionedManaged","ProvisionedGlobal"],["BatchDataZone","DataZoneBatch","GlobalBatch"]],"answer":["GlobalStandard","DataZoneStandard","ProvisionedManaged","GlobalProvisionedManaged","DataZoneBatch"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.1-3","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.1 · Deployment types and where data is processed","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about where data is processed.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"At-rest data always stays in the designated geography; **inference** location depends on the type: Global (any region), Data Zone (the US, EU or APAC zone; the EU zone follows the EU Data Boundary, which can include EFTA countries), or Standard/Regional (the customer's geography). With Global Standard and Data Zone Standard, an interruption in the primary region affects all traffic that was routed to it.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **At rest stays in the geography:** uploaded files, stored conversations and fine-tuning data for a Sweden Central resource stay in Sweden Central's geography, whatever the deployment type.\n- **The EU data zone includes EFTA:** a Data Zone Standard deployment in Germany West Central can process prompts in, for example, Norway or Switzerland, because they're part of the EU Data Boundary.\n- **Standard stays in the geography:** a Standard deployment in Japan East processes prompts within Japan.\n- **Automatic failover** is false. Routing picks a region per request when the deployment is healthy, but if the primary region has an outage, the traffic routed to it is affected. Plan your own failover across resources.\n\n**Source:** [deployment types](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Data stored at rest stays in the resource's Azure geography for every deployment type.","A Data Zone Standard deployment in an EU region can process prompts in any EU Data Boundary region, which can include Norway and Switzerland.","A Standard (regional) deployment processes prompts within the customer-specified Azure geography.","Global Standard deployments fail over to another region automatically if the primary region has an outage."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.1-4","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.1 · Deployment types and where data is processed","scenario":"","stem":"You need low **latency variance** at a large, steady volume. Which deployment category do the docs point you to?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Standard types are best-effort, and customers with high consistent volume can see greater latency variability. **Provisioned** types provide guaranteed throughput and lower latency variance. Batch has no real-time SLA, and Developer has no SLA at all.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Provisioned:** an app that sends 50 requests per second around the clock gets similar response times at 9 a.m. and 9 p.m., because the PTUs are reserved for it.\n- **Global Standard** is best-effort, shared capacity, so the same request can take 2 seconds at night and 6 during a busy hour.\n- **Global Batch** has no real-time latency at all: results arrive within 24 hours.\n- **Developer** deployments have no SLA, are meant for testing fine-tuned models, and are deleted after 24 hours.\n\n**Source:** [deployment types](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Provisioned"},{"key":"B","text":"Global Standard"},{"key":"C","text":"Global Batch"},{"key":"D","text":"Developer"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.2-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.2 · Quota: tokens and requests per minute","scenario":"","stem":"Your subscription has 240,000 TPM of quota for one model and deployment type in East US. Which allocation is allowed?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Quota is assigned **per subscription, per region, per model, per deployment type**, in tokens per minute. You hand TPM out to deployments until the quota is used up, across any number of resources in that region. That flexibility is why up to 30 resources per region are allowed and you can deploy the same model more than once in a resource.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Any split up to 240,000:** for example, `chat-prod` at 150,000 TPM in resource A, `chat-test` at 40,000 TPM in resource B and `batch-jobs` at 50,000 TPM in resource A, all in East US.\n- **Exactly one deployment** is false. Quota is a pool you divide, not a single deployment.\n- **240,000 in each resource** is false. Creating five resources doesn't give you 1.2 million TPM, because all East US resources in the subscription share one pool.\n- **Another pool for other regions:** each region has its *own* quota (West US has its own 240,000, for example), not one shared pool for \"the rest\".\n\n**Source:** [quota](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Any number of deployments, in one or more resources in East US, as long as their TPM adds up to no more than 240,000"},{"key":"B","text":"Exactly one deployment of 240,000 TPM"},{"key":"C","text":"240,000 TPM in each Azure OpenAI resource in East US"},{"key":"D","text":"240,000 TPM in East US and another 240,000 TPM shared across all other regions"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.2-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.2 · Quota: tokens and requests per minute","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about quota.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Quota comes in capacity units that map to both TPM and RPM (for older chat models, 1 unit = 1,000 TPM and 6 RPM; o-series models use different ratios), so you can't set them independently. TPM quota has nothing to do with the model's input token limit. **Cognitive Services Usages Reader** must be assigned at **subscription** scope to read the Usages API. Quota is a limit, not a reservation of capacity.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **TPM sets RPM too:** for an older chat model, a 100,000-TPM deployment (100 units) also gets 600 RPM. You can't trade TPM for more RPM.\n- **Raising TPM raises the input limit** is false. A 1-million-TPM deployment of a 128K-context model still rejects a 200,000-token prompt.\n- **Cognitive Services Usages Reader at subscription scope:** lets a FinOps person see how much quota each region and model has used, without any rights to the resources.\n- **PTU quota guarantees capacity** is false. Quota is permission to deploy up to that much. If the region is out of capacity when you create the deployment, it can still fail.\n\n**Source:** [quota](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Assigning TPM to a deployment also sets its requests-per-minute (RPM) limit, at a ratio that can differ by model.","Raising a deployment's TPM increases the model's maximum input tokens per request.","The Cognitive Services Usages Reader role at subscription scope is the least-privilege way to view quota usage.","Approved PTU quota guarantees that capacity is available in the region."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.3-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.3 · Rate limits, 429 responses and retries","scenario":"","stem":"Your app gets HTTP 429 responses from a Standard deployment. Which practices do the docs recommend? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"429s mean the deployment hit its TPM or RPM limit. Honour `retry-after-ms`, otherwise back off exponentially with jitter and cap the retries (the OpenAI Python SDK retries twice by default; raise it with `max_retries`). **Failed requests still count toward the rate limit**, so retrying without backoff makes throttling worse. Output-token limits don't reserve capacity.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`retry-after-ms`:** a 429 with `retry-after-ms: 2000` means wait two seconds, not longer and not shorter.\n- **Exponential backoff with jitter:** without the header, wait 1 s, 2 s, 4 s and so on, plus a random fraction, up to a maximum number of tries. The OpenAI SDK does this for you: `OpenAI(..., max_retries=5)` (the default is 2).\n- **Watching the headers:** `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens: 1200` on a response tells a batch job to slow down before the next call fails.\n- **A tight retry loop** is false. Failed calls count against the limit too, so hammering the deployment keeps it throttled for longer.\n- **A higher `max_tokens`** is false. It reserves nothing, and on a Standard deployment it doesn't change the limits.\n\n**Source:** [quota](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Retry after the number of milliseconds in the `retry-after-ms` header when it's present"},{"key":"B","text":"Otherwise use exponential backoff with random jitter and a maximum number of retries"},{"key":"C","text":"Watch `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` and `x-ratelimit-remaining-requests` and slow down before hitting the limit"},{"key":"D","text":"Resend the request immediately in a tight loop until it succeeds"},{"key":"E","text":"Set `max_tokens` much higher so the deployment reserves capacity for you"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.3-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.3 · Rate limits, 429 responses and retries","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about how rate limits are calculated.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Token rate limiting uses **actual** input and generated tokens, so a high output limit doesn't reserve anything. RPM is evaluated over short windows (typically 1 or 10 seconds), so bursts can be throttled. Provisioned deployments are different: they use **utilization-based** request evaluation.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Actual tokens:** a call with a 500-token prompt and `max_tokens: 4000` that generates 200 tokens counts as about 700 tokens.\n- **Short windows:** a 600-RPM deployment allows about 10 requests per second. Sending 100 requests in the first second of a minute gets 429s, even though 100 is far below 600.\n- **Shorter prompts and outputs:** trimming a 3,000-token system prompt to 800 tokens leaves room for almost three times as many calls per minute.\n- **Same as Standard** is false. Provisioned deployments throttle on *utilization* of their PTUs, not on a TPM count.\n\n**Source:** [quota](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The token rate limit counts the actual input and generated tokens, not the `max_tokens` value in the request.","RPM limits expect requests spread evenly across the minute, so a burst in 1 or 10 seconds can get 429s below the per-minute total.","Shorter prompts and limiting the output to what you need both help you stay under the limit.","Provisioned deployments use exactly the same TPM-based rate limiting as Standard deployments."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.4-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.4 · Provisioned throughput (PTUs)","scenario":"","stem":"Which statement about provisioned throughput units (PTUs) is true?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"PTUs are a fixed amount of processing capacity. Quota is **model-independent** but **region-specific** and per deployment type (Global, Data Zone and Regional Provisioned are separate pools). Heavier models need more PTUs for the same TPM, and each model has a minimum PTU count per deployment.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Model-independent, TPM varies:** with 300 PTUs of Global Provisioned quota, you could deploy 300 PTUs of `gpt-4o` or split them between `gpt-4o` and `gpt-4o-mini`. The mini model gets far more tokens per minute per PTU.\n- **PTUs for one model and version** is false. Quota isn't tied to a model, and you choose the model when you create the deployment.\n- **East US quota covers West Europe** is false. PTU quota is per region.\n- **One shared pool for Global, Data Zone and Regional** is false. Each provisioned type has its own quota.\n\n**Source:** [provisioned throughput](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"PTU quota is model-independent: the same quota can deploy any supported model, but the TPM a PTU delivers depends on the model"},{"key":"B","text":"You buy PTUs for one specific model and version"},{"key":"C","text":"PTU quota in East US also covers deployments in West Europe"},{"key":"D","text":"Global, Data Zone and Regional Provisioned share one PTU quota pool"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.4-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.4 · Provisioned throughput (PTUs)","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about provisioned deployments and their billing.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Provisioned billing is **hourly per PTU**, or discounted through **Azure Reservations** (1-month or 1-year), which are a billing discount only and don't guarantee capacity: create the deployment first, then buy the reservation. Capacity is finite and changes during the day. **Spillover** routes requests that would fail (for example 429) to a standard deployment, for all requests or per request with the `x-ms-spillover-deployment` header.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Hourly from creation to deletion:** a 100-PTU deployment that receives no traffic over a weekend is still billed for every hour.\n- **A reservation guarantees capacity** is false. A reservation is only a discount on PTU hours. Create the deployment first (which secures the capacity), then buy the reservation to pay less for it.\n- **Deleting releases capacity:** delete a 200-PTU deployment on Friday, and on Monday the region might not have 200 PTUs free for you.\n- **Spillover:** with spillover set to a Global Standard deployment, a request that would get 429 from the full provisioned deployment goes to Standard and is billed per token. Per request, add the header `x-ms-spillover-deployment: chat-standard`.\n\n**Source:** [provisioned throughput](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A provisioned deployment is billed per PTU per hour from creation to deletion, whatever the token usage.","An Azure reservation guarantees that PTU capacity is available in the region.","Deleting a provisioned deployment releases its capacity, and it might not be available if you re-create it later.","Spillover can send overflow requests from a fully used provisioned deployment to a standard deployment in the same resource."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.4-3","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.4 · Provisioned throughput (PTUs)","scenario":"","stem":"You're estimating how many PTUs a workload needs. Which factors drive the sizing calculation? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Sizing converts the **request shape** (RPM, prompt and response sizes) into a normalized TPM, using the model's **output-to-input ratio** (output tokens cost more capacity) and the **cache rate** (for models before GPT-6 Astra, cached input tokens don't consume PTU capacity). Divide by the model's input TPM per PTU, or use the capacity calculator in the Foundry portal.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Request shape:** for example, 300 requests per minute with 2,000 input and 400 output tokens each.\n- **Output-to-input ratio:** output tokens take more capacity than input tokens, so the 400 output tokens weigh more than their count suggests.\n- **Cache rate:** if 60% of each prompt hits the prompt cache, those cached tokens don't use PTU capacity (for models before GPT-6 Astra), so you need fewer PTUs.\n- **The number of developers** doesn't matter: what counts is the traffic, not who wrote the app.\n- **The API version** doesn't change how much capacity a token needs.\n\nThe Foundry portal's capacity calculator takes these inputs and returns a PTU count.\n\n**Source:** [provisioned throughput](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Requests per minute with average input and output tokens"},{"key":"B","text":"The model's output-to-input token ratio"},{"key":"C","text":"The prompt cache rate"},{"key":"D","text":"The number of developers in the project"},{"key":"E","text":"The Azure OpenAI API version"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.5-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.5 · Priority and Flex processing","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the request settings. Request 1 must use the lowest-cost, delay-tolerant tier; request 2 must use the low-latency pay-as-you-go tier; request 3 must use whatever tier its deployment is configured with.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`service_tier` works in both the Responses and Chat Completions APIs. **`flex`** (preview) costs 50% less than Standard, has no latency or service SLA, and can return 429 when Flex capacity isn't available. **`priority`** gives lower, more consistent latency with a per-model target, on Global Standard or Data Zone Standard deployments. **`auto`** uses the deployment's configured tier; **`default`** forces standard processing. The response's `service_tier` field says which tier actually processed it.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`batch`** and **`economy`** aren't service tiers. Batch is a *deployment type* with its own `.jsonl` workflow. The cheap, delay-tolerant per-request tier is `flex`.\n- **`default`** forces Standard processing. It's the right value when you need an answer and Flex just returned 429.\n- **`premium`** and **`provisioned`** aren't tiers. Provisioned is a deployment type, and the low-latency pay-as-you-go tier is `priority`.\n- **`deployment`** and **`inherit`** aren't values. Following the deployment's own setting is `auto`.\n\nCheck the response: `\"service_tier\": \"flex\"` confirms which tier actually processed the request.\n\n**Source:** [priority processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/priority-processing) · [flex processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/flex-processing)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"// request 1: nightly enrichment job\n{ \"model\": \"my-deployment\", \"input\": \"...\", \"service_tier\": \"[[0]]\" }\n\n// request 2: customer-facing chat\n{ \"model\": \"my-deployment\", \"input\": \"...\", \"service_tier\": \"[[1]]\" }\n\n// request 3: follow the deployment setting\n{ \"model\": \"my-deployment\", \"input\": \"...\", \"service_tier\": \"[[2]]\" }","options":[["batch","default","economy","flex"],["default","premium","priority","provisioned"],["auto","default","deployment","inherit"]],"answer":["flex","priority","auto"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.5-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.5 · Priority and Flex processing","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Flex and Priority processing.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Flex never falls back on its own: an unsupported model returns HTTP **400 `invalid_request_error`**, and missing capacity returns **429**, so a completion-first app retries with `default`. Priority can be set on the deployment (`default` or `priority`) or per request, and it's a pay-as-you-go option on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard deployments, often combined with PTUs for steady traffic.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Falls back to Standard** is false. On a model without Flex, the request fails with **400 `invalid_request_error`**, so it never quietly runs at Standard prices.\n- **Retry with `default`:**\n\n  ```python\n  try:\n      r = client.responses.create(model=\"chat\", input=text, service_tier=\"flex\")\n  except openai.RateLimitError:\n      r = client.responses.create(model=\"chat\", input=text, service_tier=\"default\")\n  ```\n\n- **Priority on the deployment:** a PUT with `\"properties\": { \"service_tier\": \"priority\", ... }` makes every request priority, unless a request sets its own tier.\n- **Requires provisioned** is false. Priority is pay-as-you-go on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard deployments. Some teams combine PTUs for the steady base with priority for peaks.\n\n**Source:** [priority processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/priority-processing) · [flex processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/flex-processing)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["If the model doesn't support Flex, a `flex` request falls back to Standard processing automatically.","When Flex capacity is unavailable, your code must retry with `service_tier` set to `default` if it needs the answer.","Priority processing can be enabled for the whole deployment with `\"properties\": {\"service_tier\": \"priority\"}`.","Priority processing requires a provisioned deployment."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.6-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.6 · Global Batch","scenario":"","stem":"You're preparing a Global Batch job. Which statements about the input file are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Batch input, like fine-tuning data, is **`.jsonl`**. Each line has `custom_id`, `method`, `url` (for example `/v1/chat/completions` or `/v1/responses`) and `body`. Results come back out of order, so match them on `custom_id`. One file targets **one** batch deployment; use another job for another deployment. Limits include 100,000 requests and 200 MB per file (1 GB with your own storage). Microsoft recommends fewer, larger files.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nOne line of the file:\n\n```json\n{\"custom_id\": \"prod-00042\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"url\": \"/v1/chat/completions\", \"body\": {\"model\": \"batch-4o\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Classify: 'Wireless mouse, 2.4GHz'\"}]}}\n```\n\n- **`.jsonl`, one request per line:** a million products are a million lines.\n- **`custom_id`:** the output's line for `prod-00042` might come back 300,000th. Join on the id, not on position.\n- **One deployment per file:** every line says `\"model\": \"batch-4o\"`. A line naming another deployment fails validation.\n- **A different deployment per line** is false. Split the work into one file per deployment.\n- **Unlimited requests** is false: the limit is 100,000 requests and 200 MB per file (1 GB with your own storage).\n\n**Source:** [batch](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/batch)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It's a JSON Lines (`.jsonl`) file with one request per line"},{"key":"B","text":"Each line needs a `custom_id`, because results don't come back in the input order"},{"key":"C","text":"Every line must name the same Global Batch deployment in `model`"},{"key":"D","text":"Each line can target a different deployment"},{"key":"E","text":"A file can hold an unlimited number of requests"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.6-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.6 · Global Batch","scenario":"","stem":"Match each batch job status to its meaning.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"A job goes `validating` → `in_progress` → `finalizing` → `completed`. Other terminal states are `failed` (validation failed), `expired` and `cancelled`. A cancelled job keeps any partial results, and you pay for completed work. Poll the status no more often than every 60 seconds.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`validating`:** for example, a line with a bad `url` makes the job go to `failed` here, before any tokens are processed.\n- **`in_progress`:** requests are running. Check `request_counts` to see how many completed or failed so far.\n- **`finalizing`:** all requests are done, and the output and error files are being written.\n- **`cancelling`:** after `client.batches.cancel(job.id)`, requests that already finished are kept (and billed) and the rest stop. That can take up to 10 minutes.\n- **The job finished and the output file is ready** is `completed`, the status after `finalizing`. Download the results with `output_file_id`.\n\n**Source:** [batch](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/batch)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`validating`","`in_progress`","`finalizing`","`cancelling`"],"values":["A cancel was requested; this can take up to 10 minutes","Processing is done and the results are being prepared","The file is valid and requests are being processed","The input file is being checked","The job finished and the output file is ready"],"answer":["The input file is being checked","The file is valid and requests are being processed","Processing is done and the results are being prepared","A cancel was requested; this can take up to 10 minutes"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.6-3","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.6 · Global Batch","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Global Batch.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Batch has its own **enqueued token** quota: the tokens in a submitted file count against it until the job reaches a terminal state, so online workloads aren't disrupted. Microsoft recommends **dynamic quota** on batch deployments. Batch doesn't support the Assistants API or On Your Data. Setting an input file expiration (`expires_after`, 14–30 days) raises the input file limit from 500 to 10,000.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **50% off, 24-hour target:**\n\n  ```python\n  job = client.batches.create(input_file_id=f.id, endpoint=\"/chat/completions\", completion_window=\"24h\")\n  ```\n\n  costs half the Global Standard token price, and results come back within a day.\n- **Same TPM quota as online** is false. Batch uses a separate *enqueued token* quota, so a 50-million-token job doesn't slow down your chat app.\n- **Dynamic quota:** with it on, a job that needs more enqueued tokens than your fixed quota can still be accepted when capacity is free, instead of failing with *insufficient enqueued token quota*.\n- **On Your Data** is false. Batch can't ground requests on an Azure AI Search index the way On Your Data does. Put the context in each request's prompt instead.\n\n**Source:** [batch](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/batch)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Batch costs 50% less than Global Standard and targets a 24-hour turnaround.","Batch jobs consume the same TPM quota as your online Global Standard deployment.","Enabling dynamic quota on batch deployments helps avoid failures from insufficient enqueued token quota.","Batch works with Azure OpenAI On Your Data."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.7-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.7 · Latency and throughput","scenario":"","stem":"A chat app's response time went from 3 to 6 seconds. According to the latency formula, what should you check first?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**TTLT = TTFT + (TBT × tokens generated)**. Since total time scales with output length, a longer response often explains a slower one. Always pair a latency metric with a token-count metric. Each prompt token adds little time compared to each generated token.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Generated tokens:** with TTFT 0.5 s and TBT 25 ms, a 100-token answer takes 0.5 + 2.5 = 3 s, and a 220-token answer takes 0.5 + 5.5 = 6 s. A system prompt change that made answers wordier would explain it.\n- **The API version** doesn't change how fast the model generates tokens.\n- **The number of keys** has nothing to do with performance. Keys only authenticate.\n- **A changed deployment name** would make calls fail with 404, not take longer.\n\n**Source:** [latency](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/latency)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The number of generated tokens, because total time = time to first token + time between tokens × tokens generated"},{"key":"B","text":"The API version"},{"key":"C","text":"The number of keys on the resource"},{"key":"D","text":"Whether the deployment name changed"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-3.7-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.7 · Latency and throughput","scenario":"","stem":"Match each Azure Monitor metric to what it measures.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Use **Time to Response** for streaming and first-token responsiveness, **Time to Last Byte** for non-streaming or overall time, and **Time Between Tokens** for generation speed. **Normalized Time to First Byte** is only for comparing efficiency across prompt sizes, not for absolute latency. Pair them with Generated Completion Tokens and Processed Prompt Tokens.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Time to Response:** for a streaming chat UI, the time until the first words appear. Users feel this one.\n- **Time to Last Byte:** for a non-streaming API call, the time until the whole answer is back.\n- **Time Between Tokens:** generation speed. If it doubles, the model is slower, not just the answers longer.\n- **Normalized Time to First Byte:** first-byte time per prompt token, for comparing a 500-token prompt with a 5,000-token one fairly. It's not a user-facing latency.\n- **Number of 429 responses** isn't a latency metric. You get it from **Azure OpenAI Requests** split by status code.\n\n**Source:** [latency](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/latency)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Time to Response (`AzureOpenAITimeToResponse`)","Time to Last Byte (`AzureOpenAITTLTInMS`)","Time Between Tokens (`AzureOpenAINormalizedTBTInMS`)","Normalized Time to First Byte (`AzureOpenAINormalizedTTFTInMS`)"],"values":["Average time between generated tokens (TBT)","First-byte latency divided by prompt tokens, for comparing prompts of different sizes","Number of 429 responses","Time to the first response chunk (TTFT)","Total time to the last token (TTLT)"],"answer":["Time to the first response chunk (TTFT)","Total time to the last token (TTLT)","Average time between generated tokens (TBT)","First-byte latency divided by prompt tokens, for comparing prompts of different sizes"]}},{"id":"aoai-3.7-3","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 3.7 · Latency and throughput","scenario":"","stem":"Which statements about improving latency are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Streaming improves **perceived** latency (tokens arrive as soon as they're ready) and keeps long calls alive, but the total time is unchanged; it matters less for bulk tasks like translation. Content filtering adds latency. Quota is only admission logic: per-call latency means you might not reach the quota's throughput. Model choice and output size matter most.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Streaming cuts time to first token:** with `stream=True`, the first words show after 0.5 s instead of the user waiting 6 s for all of them. The last token still arrives at about 6 s.\n- **Avoids client timeouts:** a 3-minute generation behind a proxy with a 60-second idle timeout survives when streamed, because bytes keep flowing.\n- **Content filtering adds latency:** each prompt and completion is checked, which is a small cost for the safety it gives.\n- **Streaming finishes sooner** is false. Total generation time is the same. Streaming changes when you *see* tokens, not how fast they're made.\n- **Quota guarantees throughput** is false. 300,000 TPM of quota is a ceiling. If each call is slow, a single-threaded app might reach only a fraction of it.\n\n**Source:** [latency](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/latency)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Streaming (`stream: true`) reduces the time to the first token but not the time to get all tokens"},{"key":"B","text":"Streaming helps long calls avoid client-side timeouts"},{"key":"C","text":"Content filtering improves safety but adds latency"},{"key":"D","text":"Streaming makes the whole response finish sooner"},{"key":"E","text":"Quota directly guarantees the throughput a Standard deployment achieves"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-4.1-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.1 · API keys and authentication headers","scenario":"","stem":"Match each HTTP header to when you use it with Foundry Tools (Azure AI services).","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Foundry Tools accept a resource key in `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key`, or a token in `Authorization: Bearer …`. `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Region` is only needed when a Foundry (multi-service) key is used with Translator. Azure OpenAI's own REST examples send the key in an `api-key` header.\n\n**Source:** [authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/authentication)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key`","`Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Region`","`Authorization`"],"values":["Send `Bearer <token>` for an access token or Microsoft Entra ID","Send a resource key","Send the deployment name","Send the resource's region with a Foundry resource key, only for Azure Translator"],"answer":["Send a resource key","Send the resource's region with a Foundry resource key, only for Azure Translator","Send `Bearer <token>` for an access token or Microsoft Entra ID"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.1-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.1 · API keys and authentication headers","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about resource keys.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Keys are in the portal under **Keys and Endpoint** (or `az cognitiveservices account keys list`). Two keys allow rotation without downtime. Some services (for example Translator and Speech) accept short-lived tokens exchanged from a key: they're JWTs valid for **10 minutes**. When you standardize on Entra ID, **disable local authentication** so nobody can fall back to keys.\n\n**Source:** [authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/authentication)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Every resource has two keys, `KEY1` and `KEY2`, and either one works.","Having two keys lets you rotate one while apps keep using the other.","A token obtained by exchanging a resource key at `/sts/v1.0/issueToken` is valid for 10 minutes.","If your organization uses Microsoft Entra ID, the docs recommend disabling local (key) authentication."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.2-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and managed identities","scenario":"","stem":"Calls with a Microsoft Entra token to `https://eastus.api.cognitive.microsoft.com/` fail with **401 Unauthorized**, while the same token works for a colleague. What's the likely cause?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Microsoft Entra authentication **always needs the custom subdomain** of the resource; regional endpoints don't support it. A missing role gives **403**, not 401, and role assignments can take up to five minutes to propagate. A 404 on a valid endpoint usually means the `model` value isn't a deployment name.\n\n**Source:** [managed identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/managed-identity)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Entra authentication needs the resource's custom subdomain endpoint (`https://<name>.openai.azure.com`); regional endpoints don't support it"},{"key":"B","text":"The token was requested for the wrong tenant"},{"key":"C","text":"The identity doesn't have a role on the resource"},{"key":"D","text":"Entra tokens only work with the Responses API"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-4.2-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and managed identities","scenario":"","stem":"Match each identity to the situation the docs recommend it for.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Managed identities have no secret to store or rotate. A service principal needs a client secret or certificate that you rotate. `DefaultAzureCredential` tries a chain of credentials, so the same code uses your `az login` session locally and the managed identity in Azure.\n\n**Source:** [managed identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/managed-identity)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Your developer account (`az login`)","System-assigned managed identity","User-assigned managed identity","Service principal"],"values":["Anonymous public access","CI/CD pipelines and hosts outside Azure","Local development","One Azure resource that needs access, tied to its lifecycle","Several resources sharing the same access, or granting the role before the resource exists"],"answer":["Local development","One Azure resource that needs access, tied to its lifecycle","Several resources sharing the same access, or granting the role before the resource exists","CI/CD pipelines and hosts outside Azure"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.2-3","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and managed identities","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the Python code so the same app calls Azure OpenAI with Microsoft Entra ID both on a developer laptop (after `az login`) and on an Azure host with a managed identity, without an API key.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`get_bearer_token_provider` returns a callable that fetches and refreshes tokens for the data-plane audience `https://ai.azure.com/.default`. The OpenAI client's **`api_key`** parameter accepts a string or that callable, and sends the token as `Authorization: Bearer`. `model` is your **deployment name**. The control plane (management operations) uses a different audience, `https://management.azure.com/.default`; a token for one audience doesn't work with the other.\n\n**Source:** [managed identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/managed-identity)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"from openai import OpenAI\nfrom azure.identity import (DefaultAzureCredential, ClientSecretCredential,\n                            get_bearer_token_provider)\n\ntoken_provider = get_bearer_token_provider(\n    [[0]](), \"[[1]]\")\n\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=\"https://my-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/\",\n    [[2]]=token_provider)\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"[[3]]\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello\"}])","values":["ClientSecretCredential","DefaultAzureCredential","api_key","azure_ad_token","gpt-4o-model-family","https://ai.azure.com/.default","https://management.azure.com/.default","my-chat-deployment"],"answer":["DefaultAzureCredential","https://ai.azure.com/.default","api_key","my-chat-deployment"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.2-4","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and managed identities","scenario":"","stem":"An app works locally with `DefaultAzureCredential` but fails in Azure. The host has two user-assigned managed identities. What fixes it while keeping the same code?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"With several identities on a host, the credential can't guess which one to use. Pass the client ID to `ManagedIdentityCredential`, or set **`AZURE_CLIENT_ID`** so `DefaultAzureCredential` selects it. Also make sure the role was given to the identity, not just to your user account.\n\n**Source:** [managed identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/managed-identity)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Set the `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` environment variable on the host to the client ID of the identity to use"},{"key":"B","text":"Assign the Cognitive Services OpenAI User role to your own account"},{"key":"C","text":"Switch the resource to a regional endpoint"},{"key":"D","text":"Add `api-version=latest` to the endpoint"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-4.3-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.3 · Azure RBAC roles for Azure OpenAI","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the least-privileged built-in role.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**OpenAI User**: inference with Entra ID, playgrounds, view deployments. **OpenAI Contributor**: plus create/edit deployments, fine-tune, upload training data, view stored completions. **Cognitive Services Contributor**: create resources, view and regenerate keys, create custom guardrails. **Usages Reader** (subscription scope only): view quota.\n\n**Source:** [role based access control](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/role-based-access-control)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["An app's managed identity only calls chat and embeddings with Entra ID","A developer creates deployments and fine-tuning jobs but must not see the keys","An admin creates Azure OpenAI resources and regenerates keys","A finance analyst views quota usage across the subscription"],"values":["Cognitive Services Contributor","Cognitive Services OpenAI Contributor","Cognitive Services OpenAI User","Cognitive Services Usages Reader","Owner"],"answer":["Cognitive Services OpenAI User","Cognitive Services OpenAI Contributor","Cognitive Services Contributor","Cognitive Services Usages Reader"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.3-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.3 · Azure RBAC roles for Azure OpenAI","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Azure OpenAI roles.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Cognitive Services Contributor is a management role: it can create resources and manage keys, but it **can't make Entra ID inference calls** (combine it with OpenAI User if needed). OpenAI User and OpenAI Contributor can't see keys. Usages Reader exists only at subscription level. Subscription-level Owner and Contributor are inherited and take priority over OpenAI roles assigned lower down.\n\n**Source:** [role based access control](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/role-based-access-control)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Cognitive Services Contributor can make inference calls with Microsoft Entra ID.","Cognitive Services OpenAI User can view and copy the API keys.","Cognitive Services Usages Reader can only be assigned at subscription scope.","Cognitive Services OpenAI Contributor can create and edit model deployments."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.4-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.4 · Firewalls, virtual networks and private endpoints","scenario":"","stem":"You turn on the firewall for an Azure OpenAI resource (**Selected Networks and Private Endpoints**) without adding any rules. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Selecting selected networks with no rules **denies all access** to data, but management through the portal, PowerShell and CLI still works. Requests from other Azure services, the portal's test consoles and logging services are blocked unless allowed. IP rules accept only **public** addresses (no `10.*`, `172.16–31.*`, `192.168.*`) and don't support `/31` or `/32` ranges; use single-IP rules for those.\n\n**Source:** [cognitive services virtual networks](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/cognitive-services-virtual-networks) · [configure private link](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/how-to/configure-private-link)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["All data-plane requests are now blocked.","You can still configure the resource through the Azure portal, PowerShell or the Azure CLI.","Requests from other Azure services are allowed automatically.","IP rules can allow private ranges such as `10.0.0.0/8`."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.4-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.4 · Firewalls, virtual networks and private endpoints","scenario":"","stem":"An app in a VNet subnet must reach Azure OpenAI over a **service endpoint**, with all other traffic denied. What do you configure? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Enable the **`Microsoft.CognitiveServices`** service endpoint on the subnet, add a **VNet rule** for it, and make sure the **default action is deny**, otherwise network rules have no effect. Requests from the VNet must use the resource's **custom subdomain**. Private address ranges aren't allowed in IP rules. The subnet can even be in another subscription or tenant.\n\n**Source:** [cognitive services virtual networks](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/cognitive-services-virtual-networks) · [configure private link](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/how-to/configure-private-link)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Enable the `Microsoft.CognitiveServices` service endpoint on the subnet"},{"key":"B","text":"Add a virtual network rule for that subnet on the Azure OpenAI resource"},{"key":"C","text":"Set the default network rule to deny"},{"key":"D","text":"Call the endpoint through its custom subdomain"},{"key":"E","text":"Add the subnet's private address range as an IP rule"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-4.4-3","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.4 · Firewalls, virtual networks and private endpoints","scenario":"","stem":"You add a private endpoint for an Azure OpenAI resource and disable public access. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Private endpoints use the **same connection string**: the custom subdomain URL. Azure changes the CNAME to a `privatelink` alias and creates a private DNS zone with the A record, so the name resolves to the private IP inside the VNet and the public IP outside. Never call `*.privatelink.openai.azure.com` directly. A custom DNS server must forward the `privatelink` zone to Azure DNS (168.63.129.16) or hold the A records.\n\n**Source:** [cognitive services virtual networks](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/cognitive-services-virtual-networks) · [configure private link](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/how-to/configure-private-link)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Clients in the VNet should call `https://<name>.openai.azure.com`, the same URL as before.","Clients should call the internal `*.privatelink.openai.azure.com` URL directly.","From inside the VNet, the endpoint's DNS name resolves to the private endpoint's IP address.","With a custom DNS server, you must forward or delegate the `privatelink` zone so the name resolves privately."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.5-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.5 · Encryption with customer-managed keys","scenario":"","stem":"You enable customer-managed keys for an Azure OpenAI resource. Which requirements apply? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The key vault must be in the **same region and tenant** (a different subscription is fine), with **soft delete and purge protection**. If the vault firewall is on, allow trusted Microsoft services. Give the resource's managed identity **get, wrap key, unwrap key** (access policies) or the **Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User** role (RBAC). Only **RSA / RSA-HSM 2048** keys are supported.\n\n**Source:** [encrypt data at rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/encrypt-data-at-rest)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The key vault and the resource are in the same region and Microsoft Entra tenant"},{"key":"B","text":"Soft delete and purge protection are enabled on the key vault"},{"key":"C","text":"The resource's managed identity can get, wrap and unwrap the key (for example with Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User)"},{"key":"D","text":"The key is an RSA or RSA-HSM key of size 2048"},{"key":"E","text":"The key vault is in the same subscription as the resource"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-4.6-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.6 · Data, privacy and stored content","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about your data in models sold by Azure, including Azure OpenAI.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Prompts, completions, embeddings and training data aren't available to other customers or to OpenAI and other providers, and aren't used to improve their models or to train foundation models without your permission. Azure OpenAI doesn't interact with services operated by OpenAI. The models are stateless, and fine-tuned models are exclusive to you.\n\n**Source:** [data privacy](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/responsible-ai/openai/data-privacy)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Your prompts and completions are available to OpenAI.","Your prompts and completions are used to train or improve the base models.","The models themselves are stateless: prompts and completions aren't stored in the model.","Your fine-tuned models are available only to you."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-4.6-2","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 4.6 · Data, privacy and stored content","scenario":"","stem":"Which features store data in the service, at rest in your resource's geography? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Stateful features store data: the **Responses API** (message history), the Assistants API threads, **stored completions**, and **uploaded files** (fine-tuning, batch, vector stores). That data stays in your resource's geography, is encrypted with AES-256 (optionally also with a customer-managed key) and can be deleted at any time. A plain inference call isn't stored by the model, though abuse monitoring might sample prompts for review.\n\n**Source:** [data privacy](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/responsible-ai/openai/data-privacy)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The Responses API, for message history"},{"key":"B","text":"Stored completions (preview)"},{"key":"C","text":"Files uploaded for fine-tuning or batch"},{"key":"D","text":"A stateless chat completions call with no stored completions"},{"key":"E","text":"The base model's weights"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.1-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.1 · The v1 API and API versions","scenario":"","stem":"You move an app to the v1 Azure OpenAI API with key authentication. Complete the client code.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"With the v1 API you use the standard **`OpenAI()`** client (not `AzureOpenAI()`), set `base_url` to your endpoint plus **`/openai/v1/`**, and you no longer need an `api-version`. The `OpenAI()` client also handles Entra token refresh when you pass a token provider as `api_key`. `base_url` can also use the `https://<name>.services.ai.azure.com/openai/v1/` form.\n\n**Source:** [api version lifecycle](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/api-version-lifecycle)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"import os\nimport openai\n\nclient = openai.[[0]](\n    api_key=os.getenv(\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"),\n    base_url=\"https://my-resource.openai.azure.com[[1]]\")\n\nresponse = client.responses.create(model=\"my-deployment\", input=\"This is a test.\")","options":[["AzureOpenAI","AzureOpenAIv1","OpenAI"],["/openai/deployments/my-deployment/","/openai/v1/","/v1/?api-version=2024-10-21"]],"answer":["OpenAI","/openai/v1/"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.1-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.1 · The v1 API and API versions","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about the v1 API.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Since August 2025 the **v1** routes (`/openai/v1/...`) replace monthly dated `api-version` values. Preview APIs are opted into with headers, or are marked by `alpha` in the path (for example `/openai/v1/fine_tuning/alpha/graders/`). The v1 API supports cross-provider chat completions, although Microsoft recommends the Responses API for Azure OpenAI models.\n\n**Source:** [api version lifecycle](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/api-version-lifecycle)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["You need to update the `api-version` every month to get new features.","Preview features are enabled with feature-specific headers or an `alpha` path segment, without changing API version.","The v1 chat completions route can call models from other providers that support the v1 syntax, such as DeepSeek and Grok.","You should parse only the response fields you need, because new fields can be added at any time."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.2-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.2 · Chat Completions: messages and roles","scenario":"","stem":"Which `messages` array asks the model to answer a new question after showing it one example question and answer?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Chat models take a transcript of messages by role. The optional **system** message comes first; example **user**/**assistant** pairs give few-shot examples; the array **ends with a user message** so it's the assistant's turn. Developer messages (`role: developer`) are functionally the same as system messages for reasoning models.\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`[{system: instructions}, {user: example question}, {assistant: example answer}, {user: new question}]`"},{"key":"B","text":"`[{user: example question}, {user: example answer}, {system: new question}]`"},{"key":"C","text":"`[{system: instructions}, {assistant: new question}]`"},{"key":"D","text":"`[{assistant: example answer}, {system: instructions}, {user: new question}, {assistant: \"\"}]`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.2-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.2 · Chat Completions: messages and roles","scenario":"","stem":"What can you put in the system message, according to the chat completions guidance? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The system message sets the assistant's description, personality, rules and small amounts of context. For a large amount of data, retrieve only the relevant parts at query time with embeddings or Azure AI Search instead. Never put secrets in a prompt. A system message is optional, but include at least a basic one for best results.\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A brief description of the assistant and its personality"},{"key":"B","text":"Rules the assistant must follow"},{"key":"C","text":"A small amount of grounding data, such as FAQ entries"},{"key":"D","text":"Your API key so the model can call other services"},{"key":"E","text":"Large document collections that don't fit in the context window"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.2-3","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.2 · Chat Completions: messages and roles","scenario":"","stem":"A long-running chat keeps appending messages until requests start failing. Whose job is it to keep the conversation within the model's limit with the Chat Completions API?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"With Chat Completions, the combined tokens of `messages` plus the requested output must fit the model's limit, and **managing that is your responsibility** (for example, counting tokens with tiktoken and dropping old turns). The Responses API can manage conversation history and truncation for you.\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Your application's: track the token count and trim the messages so prompt plus requested output fits the context window"},{"key":"B","text":"The service's: Chat Completions drops the oldest messages automatically"},{"key":"C","text":"Nobody's: the context window grows with each turn"},{"key":"D","text":"The model's: it summarizes old turns on its own"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.3-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.3 · Chat Completions: parameters, responses and errors","scenario":"","stem":"Match each `finish_reason` value to its meaning.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Every choice has a `finish_reason`. `function_call` is the deprecated form of `tool_calls`. When streaming, `finish_reason` is `null` until the final chunk. If you see `length`, raise `max_completion_tokens`.\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt) · [chat](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/microsoft-foundry/azureopenai/chat)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`stop`","`length`","`content_filter`","`tool_calls`"],"values":["Content was omitted because a content filter flagged it","The model called a tool","The model finished its output","The output was cut off by `max_completion_tokens` or the token limit","The request was throttled"],"answer":["The model finished its output","The output was cut off by `max_completion_tokens` or the token limit","Content was omitted because a content filter flagged it","The model called a tool"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.3-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.3 · Chat Completions: parameters, responses and errors","scenario":"","stem":"Match each Chat Completions request parameter to what it does.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`n` ranges 1–128 and every choice costs tokens, so keep it at 1. Both penalties range from −2.0 to 2.0. `logit_bias` maps token IDs to −100…100. `max_completion_tokens` replaces the deprecated `max_tokens` and covers visible and reasoning tokens. `user` is deprecated in favour of `safety_identifier` (abuse monitoring) and `prompt_cache_key` (caching).\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt) · [chat](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/microsoft-foundry/azureopenai/chat)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`n`","`presence_penalty`","`frequency_penalty`","`logit_bias`","`max_completion_tokens`"],"values":["Caps generated tokens, including reasoning tokens","Encourages new topics by penalizing tokens that already appeared","How many choices to generate (each one is billed)","Raises or lowers the likelihood of specific token IDs (−100 to 100)","Reduces verbatim repetition by penalizing tokens by how often they appeared","Sets the deployment's TPM"],"answer":["How many choices to generate (each one is billed)","Encourages new topics by penalizing tokens that already appeared","Reduces verbatim repetition by penalizing tokens by how often they appeared","Raises or lowers the likelihood of specific token IDs (−100 to 100)","Caps generated tokens, including reasoning tokens"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.3-3","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.3 · Chat Completions: parameters, responses and errors","scenario":"","stem":"Your code gets these results. Answer Yes or No for each likely explanation.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"These are the common errors listed for chat completions. For the 500 invalid-Unicode error, the documented workaround is a temperature below 1 plus retry logic. The response also carries `usage` (`prompt_tokens`, `completion_tokens`, `total_tokens`) and content filter results for both the prompt and each choice.\n\n**Source:** [chatgpt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/chatgpt) · [chat](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/microsoft-foundry/azureopenai/chat)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A 404 or 400 'deployment not found' error usually means `model` doesn't match a deployment name.","An 'invalid URL' error with the v1 API can mean `base_url` doesn't end with `/openai/v1/`.","A 401 or 403 error points to authentication or authorization.","A 500 'model generated invalid Unicode output' error can often be fixed by retrying, with temperature below 1."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.4-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.4 · Responses API: state, chaining and compaction","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the code so the second request continues the conversation that the service stored.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The Responses API takes `input` (a string or an array of items), and **`previous_response_id`** chains a turn onto a stored response so you don't resend history. `output_text` is a convenience property with the generated text. `messages` and `choices[0].message.content` belong to Chat Completions.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"first = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"my-deployment\",\n    [[0]]=\"Define catastrophic forgetting.\")\n\nsecond = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"my-deployment\",\n    [[1]]=first.[[2]],\n    input=\"Explain it for a college freshman.\")\n\nprint(second.[[3]])","values":["choices[0].message.content","conversation_id","id","input","messages","output_text","previous_response_id"],"answer":["input","previous_response_id","id","output_text"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.4-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.4 · Responses API: state, chaining and compaction","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about stored responses.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Responses are stored for **30 days** by default; `GET` and `DELETE /responses/{id}` retrieve or remove one, and you can list a response's input items. With **`store=false`** (stateless), your app resends the output items in `input` instead; `previous_response_id` only works with stored responses.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["By default, response data is retained for 30 days.","You can retrieve or delete a stored response by its ID.","With `store=false`, you continue the conversation with `previous_response_id`.","Stored Responses API content is encrypted at rest, with a customer-managed key if one is configured for the resource."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.4-3","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.4 · Responses API: state, chaining and compaction","scenario":"","stem":"A long agent conversation is running out of context. You want the service to shrink the history automatically when output crosses a threshold. What do you set?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Compaction** reduces the input context while keeping essential state in an opaque, encrypted compaction item. Call `responses.compact` yourself, or use **server-side compaction** with `context_management` and a `compact_threshold`. Server-side compaction also works with `store=false`. With stateless chaining you can drop items before the latest compaction item; with `previous_response_id` don't prune manually.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`context_management` with a `compact_threshold` on `responses.create`"},{"key":"B","text":"`truncation: \"disabled\"`"},{"key":"C","text":"`max_output_tokens: 0`"},{"key":"D","text":"`n: 2`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.5-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.5 · Responses API: streaming, background tasks and MCP","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about background tasks in the Responses API.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Background mode runs long reasoning tasks asynchronously; you poll, stream or cancel by ID. It **requires `store=true`**. If a stream drops, a `GET` on the response with `stream=true&starting_after=N` replays the events after sequence number N. To cancel a synchronous response, close the connection.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Setting `background=true` returns immediately with a response ID and a `queued` status that you poll.","Background mode works with `store=false`.","You can resume a dropped stream with `starting_after=<sequence_number>` if the original request used `stream=true`.","Background mode is meant for long tasks with reasoning models, such as Deep Research-style agents."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.5-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.5 · Responses API: streaming, background tasks and MCP","scenario":"","stem":"You attach a remote MCP server as a tool in a Responses API request. By default, what happens before data is sent to that server?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"By default the Responses API **requires explicit approval** before sharing data with a remote MCP server: it returns an `mcp_approval_request` you can inspect, and you continue by approving it. Microsoft recommends reviewing and logging what's shared. MCP is the open standard for exposing tools and context to models.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The response contains an `mcp_approval_request` item, and data is shared only after you approve it"},{"key":"B","text":"Data is sent immediately; approvals must be turned on explicitly"},{"key":"C","text":"The request fails unless the MCP server is in the same Azure region"},{"key":"D","text":"The model refuses to call external tools"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.5-3","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.5 · Responses API: streaming, background tasks and MCP","scenario":"","stem":"Which capabilities does the Responses API offer that the Chat Completions API doesn't? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The Responses API combines Chat Completions and the Assistants API: stored state, built-in tools (Code Interpreter with containers, remote MCP, image generation, computer use), background tasks and compaction. Both APIs support function calling and streaming. Microsoft recommends the Responses API for new apps.\n\n**Source:** [responses](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Server-stored, multi-turn state chained with `previous_response_id`"},{"key":"B","text":"Built-in tools such as Code Interpreter and remote MCP servers"},{"key":"C","text":"Background tasks for long-running requests"},{"key":"D","text":"Function calling"},{"key":"E","text":"Streaming"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.6-1","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.6 · Reasoning models","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about reasoning tokens.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Reasoning tokens are hidden but take context space and are billed as output; see `completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` or `output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens`. If the cap is hit, `status` is `incomplete` with `incomplete_details.reason: max_output_tokens`, possibly before any visible output. Microsoft suggests reserving at least 25,000 tokens for reasoning and output while you learn a workload.\n\n**Source:** [reasoning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Reasoning tokens appear in the message content.","Reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens.","`max_completion_tokens` (Chat Completions) and `max_output_tokens` (Responses) include reasoning tokens.","A response can come back `incomplete` with no visible answer, and you still pay for the input and reasoning tokens."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-5.6-2","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.6 · Reasoning models","scenario":"","stem":"You switch a Chat Completions app from `gpt-4o` to a reasoning model (not GPT-6 Astra). Which request parameters must you remove or replace? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Reasoning models don't support `temperature`, `top_p`, `presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`, `logprobs`, `top_logprobs`, `logit_bias` or `max_tokens`. Use **`max_completion_tokens`** instead, and control thinking with **`reasoning_effort`** (values such as `none`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, varying by model).\n\n**Source:** [reasoning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`temperature`"},{"key":"B","text":"`top_p`"},{"key":"C","text":"`max_tokens` (use `max_completion_tokens`)"},{"key":"D","text":"`presence_penalty`"},{"key":"E","text":"`messages`"},{"key":"F","text":"`reasoning_effort`"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.6-3","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.6 · Reasoning models","scenario":"","stem":"A Chat Completions request with function `tools` to a `gpt-5.6` deployment fails with \"Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported … in /v1/chat/completions\". What's the recommended fix?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"On `gpt-5.6` and the GPT-6 models, Chat Completions limits which `reasoning_effort` values can be combined with function tools. The recommended path is the **Responses API**, which supports every effort level with tools and returns reasoning items you carry across turns. Staying on Chat Completions means setting `reasoning_effort` to `none` (losing reasoning quality), and some GPT-6 models only support function tools through the Responses API.\n\n**Source:** [reasoning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Send tool-calling requests to the Responses API"},{"key":"B","text":"Lower `temperature` to 0"},{"key":"C","text":"Switch the deployment to Provisioned"},{"key":"D","text":"Remove the system message"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-5.6-4","domain":3,"section":"5","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 5.6 · Reasoning models","scenario":"","stem":"Your app uses the Responses API with `store=false` and a reasoning model that calls functions. How do you keep the model's reasoning across the function call?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"With stateless requests, the service returns **encrypted reasoning items**; your app stores them securely and replays them (with the function call and its output) in the next `input`, still with `store=false`. Don't use `previous_response_id` for an unstored response. To *see* reasoning, use the `reasoning.summary` parameter (`auto`, `concise` or `detailed`); trying to extract raw reasoning another way can violate the Acceptable Use Policy.\n\n**Source:** [reasoning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Pass back every output item in order, including reasoning items with `encrypted_content`, in the next request's `input`"},{"key":"B","text":"Use `previous_response_id`"},{"key":"C","text":"Ask the model to print its reasoning in the answer"},{"key":"D","text":"Set `reasoning_effort` to `max`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-6.1-1","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.1 · Function calling: the request and response loop","scenario":"","stem":"You include a `get_current_time` function in a chat completions request. Who runs the function?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"With function calling the model decides whether to call a function and returns `tool_calls` with the name and **JSON arguments**. **Your app** executes the function and sends the result back in another request, so you stay in control. The loop: call with tools → run the requested functions → call again with their results → get the final answer.\n\n**Source:** [function calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/function-calling)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Your application: the model only returns the function name and JSON arguments, and you send the result back"},{"key":"B","text":"The model runs it inside Azure OpenAI"},{"key":"C","text":"Azure Functions, automatically"},{"key":"D","text":"The Foundry portal, after approval"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-6.1-2","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.1 · Function calling: the request and response loop","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the code that sends a function's result back to the model after it returned a tool call.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Each tool is `{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {name, description, parameters}}`, where `parameters` is a JSON Schema. The model's message lists **`tool_calls`**, each with an `id`. Append the assistant message, then one **`role: \"tool\"`** message per call with the matching **`tool_call_id`**. With parallel calls you add one tool message per call.\n\n**Source:** [function calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/function-calling)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"tools = [{\n    \"type\": \"[[0]]\",\n    \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_current_time\",\n        \"description\": \"Get the current time in a given location\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                       \"properties\": {\"location\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                       \"required\": [\"location\"]}}}]\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(model=deployment, messages=messages,\n                                          tools=tools, tool_choice=\"auto\")\nmsg = response.choices[0].message\nmessages.append(msg)\nfor call in msg.[[1]]:\n    args = json.loads(call.function.arguments)\n    messages.append({\n        \"role\": \"[[2]]\",\n        \"[[3]]\": call.id,\n        \"name\": \"get_current_time\",\n        \"content\": get_current_time(args[\"location\"])})\n\nfinal = client.chat.completions.create(model=deployment, messages=messages)","values":["assistant","call_id","function","function_call","tool","tool_call_id","tool_calls","tool_result"],"answer":["function","tool_calls","tool","tool_call_id"]}},{"id":"aoai-6.1-3","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.1 · Function calling: the request and response loop","scenario":"","stem":"Match each `tool_choice` value to its effect.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`tool_choice` replaces the deprecated `function_call`, and `tools` replaces `functions`. Parallel function calling (several `tool_calls` in one response) is controlled separately by `parallel_tool_calls`, which defaults to true where the model supports it.\n\n**Source:** [function calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/function-calling)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`\"auto\"`","`\"none\"`","`\"required\"`","`{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_current_time\"}}`"],"values":["The model answers with a message and calls no tool","The model calls every tool in parallel","The model decides whether to call a tool, and which one (the default)","The model must call one or more tools","The model must call that specific function"],"answer":["The model decides whether to call a tool, and which one (the default)","The model answers with a message and calls no tool","The model must call one or more tools","The model must call that specific function"]}},{"id":"aoai-6.2-1","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.2 · Function calling: prompts and responsible use","scenario":"","stem":"The model isn't calling your `search_hotels` function when expected and sometimes guesses missing values. What do the docs suggest? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Function definitions are injected into the system message, so they **consume prompt tokens** and respond to prompt engineering: clear descriptions, system-message guidance on when to call, and an instruction to ask clarifying questions. If the model invents functions you didn't define, add \"Only use the functions you have been provided with.\" Descriptions are limited to 1,024 characters.\n\n**Source:** [function calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/function-calling)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Write a meaningful function `description` and describe non-obvious parameters, with format examples"},{"key":"B","text":"Tell the model in the system message when to call `search_hotels`"},{"key":"C","text":"Add \"Don't make assumptions about what values to use with functions. Ask for clarification if a user request is ambiguous.\" to the system message"},{"key":"D","text":"Remove the parameter descriptions to save tokens"},{"key":"E","text":"Raise the temperature to 2"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-6.2-2","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.2 · Function calling: prompts and responsible use","scenario":"","stem":"A function lets the model update rows in a database. Which practices do the docs recommend for using function calling responsibly? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Validate calls, use **trusted** data and tools (untrusted output can steer later calls), apply **least privilege** (for example read-only database access for query functions), consider real-world impact, and add **user confirmation** for actions. Excluding capabilities from the definition isn't a security control on its own.\n\n**Source:** [function calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/function-calling)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Validate every function call: the function, its parameters, and that it matches the intended action"},{"key":"B","text":"Give the function only the minimum database permissions it needs"},{"key":"C","text":"Ask the user to confirm before running functions that take actions"},{"key":"D","text":"Rely on leaving dangerous capabilities out of the function definition as your only security control"},{"key":"E","text":"Feed untrusted web content into function outputs without checks"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-6.3-1","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.3 · Structured outputs","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the request so the model's answer is guaranteed to match your JSON Schema.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Structured outputs use `response_format: {\"type\": \"json_schema\", …}` in Chat Completions and **`text.format`** in the Responses API. The schema rules: **every field must be in `required`** (emulate optional with a union such as `[\"string\", \"null\"]`), and **`additionalProperties` must be `false`**. With the SDK you can pass a Pydantic model to `parse` (`text_format` for Responses, result in `output_parsed`).\n\n**Source:** [structured outputs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"# Chat Completions\nclient.chat.completions.create(model=deployment, messages=messages,\n    response_format={\"type\": \"[[0]]\", \"json_schema\": {\n        \"name\": \"CalendarEvent\", \"strict\": True, \"schema\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\"name\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                           \"date\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                           \"participants\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}},\n            \"required\": [[1]],\n            \"additionalProperties\": [[2]]}}})\n\n# Responses API: the schema goes in [[3]]","options":[["json_object","json_schema","text"],["[\"name\", \"date\", \"participants\"]","[\"name\"]","[]"],["False","True","{\"type\": \"string\"}"],["instructions","response_format","text.format"]],"answer":["json_schema","[\"name\", \"date\", \"participants\"]","False","text.format"]}},{"id":"aoai-6.3-2","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.3 · Structured outputs","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about structured outputs.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Structured outputs support a subset of JSON Schema: string, number, boolean, integer, object, array, enum and `anyOf` (but the root can't be `anyOf`); `$defs` and recursive schemas work. Type-specific keywords such as `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`, `format`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `minItems` and `uniqueItems` aren't supported. Strict function calling doesn't support parallel calls.\n\n**Source:** [structured outputs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Keywords such as `minLength`, `pattern`, `minimum` and `maxItems` are supported.","A schema can have up to 100 object properties in total and up to five levels of nesting.","The output keys follow the order of the schema.","You can use structured outputs for function calling by setting `strict: true`, with `parallel_tool_calls` set to `false`."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-6.3-3","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.3 · Structured outputs","scenario":"","stem":"Your extraction app must always return JSON that matches an exact schema. Which feature should you use?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**JSON mode** guarantees valid JSON but not that it matches a schema. **Structured outputs** constrain the model to your JSON Schema, and Microsoft recommends them over JSON mode when possible.\n\n**Source:** [structured outputs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Structured outputs"},{"key":"B","text":"JSON mode"},{"key":"C","text":"A higher `max_completion_tokens`"},{"key":"D","text":"Reproducible output with `seed`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-6.4-1","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.4 · JSON mode","scenario":"","stem":"You use JSON mode with `response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"}`. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"JSON mode guarantees **valid JSON only**. The API rejects the request if `messages` don't contain the word \"json\", and you should instruct the model (usually in the system message) to produce JSON. If `finish_reason` is `length`, the JSON is partial: raise the token limit or shorten the prompt, and retry.\n\n**Source:** [json mode](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/json-mode)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The output is guaranteed to follow your schema.","The messages must mention JSON, or the request fails with a 400 error.","Without a JSON instruction, the model might emit an endless stream of whitespace until it hits the token limit.","Check that `finish_reason` isn't `length` before parsing, because the JSON could be cut off."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-6.5-1","domain":3,"section":"6","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 6.5 · Reproducible output (seed)","scenario":"","stem":"You set `seed=42` and keep every other parameter the same across chat completion calls. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`seed` (a preview feature, now marked deprecated in the API reference) requests best-effort deterministic sampling. Determinism isn't guaranteed; compare **`system_fingerprint`** across responses to spot backend changes. Longer outputs tend to diverge more.\n\n**Source:** [reproducible output](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/reproducible-output)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The outputs are guaranteed to be identical.","The service makes a best effort to sample deterministically.","`system_fingerprint` identifies the backend configuration, so a change in it can explain different outputs.","Larger `max_tokens` values generally make outputs less deterministic, even with a seed."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-7.1-1","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.1 · What embeddings are and cosine similarity","scenario":"","stem":"What does an embedding represent?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"An embedding is an information-dense vector of floats; the distance between two embeddings correlates with how semantically similar the inputs are. That makes embeddings useful for vector search, recommendations, classification and clustering. You can't decode an embedding back to the text.\n\n**Source:** [understand embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/understand-embeddings)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The semantic meaning of text as a vector of floating-point numbers, where similar text gives vectors that are close together"},{"key":"B","text":"A compressed copy of the text that can be decoded back to the original"},{"key":"C","text":"The token IDs of the text"},{"key":"D","text":"A hash that's identical for identical text and random otherwise"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-7.1-2","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.1 · What embeddings are and cosine similarity","scenario":"","stem":"Why do Azure OpenAI embeddings often use **cosine similarity** rather than counting shared words? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Cosine similarity is the cosine of the angle between two vectors. It ignores magnitude, so document length doesn't dominate, and it compares semantic content. Word-overlap counting grows with document size and finds false matches between unrelated long texts.\n\n**Source:** [understand embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/understand-embeddings)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors, so documents far apart by Euclidean distance because of size can still be similar"},{"key":"B","text":"Counting common words doesn't scale, because longer documents share more words even across unrelated topics"},{"key":"C","text":"Cosine similarity compares meaning captured in the vectors, not exact word overlap"},{"key":"D","text":"Cosine similarity needs no embeddings at all"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-7.2-1","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.2 · Calling the embeddings API","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the code that embeds a sentence and prints the vector's length.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Call `client.embeddings.create` with your **embedding deployment name** as `model` and the text (or an array of texts) as `input`. The vectors are in `response.data[i].embedding`. In REST it's `POST …/openai/v1/embeddings`.\n\n**Source:** [embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/embeddings)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"client = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://my-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/\",\n                api_key=token_provider)\n\nresponse = client.[[0]].create(\n    model=\"[[1]]\",\n    input=\"The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog.\")\n\nprint(len(response.[[2]][0].[[3]]))","values":["chat.completions","choices","data","embedding","embeddings","my-embedding-deployment","text-embedding-3","vector"],"answer":["embeddings","my-embedding-deployment","data","embedding"]}},{"id":"aoai-7.2-2","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.2 · Calling the embeddings API","scenario":"","stem":"You call the embeddings endpoint with Microsoft Entra ID. Which role must your identity have on the Azure OpenAI resource?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Inference, including embeddings, with Entra ID needs a data-plane role such as **Cognitive Services OpenAI User** (or OpenAI Contributor). Entra ID is recommended over keys because it avoids long-lived credentials. A 403 response means the role is missing; a 401 usually means a wrong token audience.\n\n**Source:** [embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/embeddings)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Cognitive Services OpenAI User"},{"key":"B","text":"Cognitive Services Usages Reader"},{"key":"C","text":"Reader"},{"key":"D","text":"Key Vault Crypto User"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-7.3-1","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.3 · Embedding limits and troubleshooting","scenario":"","stem":"You send one embeddings request with an array of inputs. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Limits per request: **8,192 tokens per input**, **2,048 inputs per array**, and **300,000 tokens in total** (HTTP 400 above it, so split large batches). Embedding tokens also count toward your deployment's TPM quota.\n\n**Source:** [embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/embeddings)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Each input can be up to 8,192 tokens with the current embedding models.","The array can hold up to 2,048 inputs.","The request fails with HTTP 400 if the inputs add up to more than 300,000 tokens, even if each input is under its limit.","Embedding requests don't count toward the deployment's tokens-per-minute quota."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-7.3-2","domain":3,"section":"7","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 7.3 · Embedding limits and troubleshooting","scenario":"","stem":"Match each embeddings error to the first thing to check.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"These are the troubleshooting steps listed for the v1 embeddings API. Throttling returns 429, not 400.\n\n**Source:** [embeddings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/embeddings)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["401","403","404","400"],"values":["Check that your identity has the Cognitive Services OpenAI User role","Check the endpoint includes `/openai/v1/` and `model` is a valid deployment name","Check the request body, each input's token count, the number of inputs and the total tokens","Sign in again and check the access token's audience","Wait for the quota to reset"],"answer":["Sign in again and check the access token's audience","Check that your identity has the Cognitive Services OpenAI User role","Check the endpoint includes `/openai/v1/` and `model` is a valid deployment name","Check the request body, each input's token count, the number of inputs and the total tokens"]}},{"id":"aoai-8.1-1","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.1 · Prompt components and few-shot learning","scenario":"","stem":"In the prompt \"Translate to French: *The weather is nice today.* — Today is Friday 14 March. — French translation:\", match each part to its prompt component.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Instructions** say what to do; **primary content** is the text being processed; **supporting content** influences the output without being the target (date, user name, preferences); a **cue** is a prefix that jumpstarts the output. **Examples** (one-shot or few-shot) show input/output pairs.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["\"Translate to French:\"","\"The weather is nice today.\"","\"Today is Friday 14 March.\"","\"French translation:\""],"values":["Cue","Few-shot examples","Instructions","Primary content","Supporting content"],"answer":["Instructions","Primary content","Supporting content","Cue"]}},{"id":"aoai-8.1-2","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.1 · Prompt components and few-shot learning","scenario":"","stem":"You add three input/output examples to a prompt and the model starts labelling correctly. What happened?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Few-shot learning isn't training: the examples only condition **the current inference**. In Chat Completions, few-shot examples are user/assistant message pairs. To permanently change behaviour, you'd fine-tune.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Few-shot learning: the examples condition the model for this request only; its weights don't change"},{"key":"B","text":"The model was fine-tuned on the three examples"},{"key":"C","text":"The examples were stored and will apply to all future requests"},{"key":"D","text":"The model switched to a reasoning mode"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-8.2-1","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.2 · Prompting techniques","scenario":"","stem":"Match each prompting technique to its description.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Models can show **recency bias**, so repeating instructions at the end can help. **Priming** (a cue like \"Here's a bulleted list of key points:\\n- \") shapes the format. **Breaking the task down** improves accuracy. **Clear syntax** makes intent clear and outputs easy to parse, and a separator can double as a stop sequence. These techniques aren't recommended for reasoning models.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Repeat instructions at the end","Prime the output","Break the task down","Add clear syntax"],"values":["Ask for intermediate steps, such as extracting facts before writing queries","Counter recency bias by restating the instructions after the content","End the prompt with the start of the desired answer, such as a bullet","Raise the temperature for more creative output","Use separators such as `---` and Markdown or XML to mark sections"],"answer":["Counter recency bias by restating the instructions after the content","End the prompt with the start of the desired answer, such as a bullet","Ask for intermediate steps, such as extracting facts before writing queries","Use separators such as `---` and Markdown or XML to mark sections"]}},{"id":"aoai-8.2-2","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.2 · Prompting techniques","scenario":"","stem":"You want fewer fabricated statements in a summarization app. Which techniques do the docs recommend? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Asking for **citations** means a fabrication needs two errors (the claim and the citation). Giving the model an **out** avoids forced answers, and **structured output** avoids compound statements that are hard to verify. A system message alone (\"only write true facts\") often isn't enough. Chain-of-thought prompting applies only to non-reasoning models; extracting a reasoning model's reasoning outside the reasoning summary isn't supported.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Ask the model to cite the source material for its statements"},{"key":"B","text":"Give the model an \"out\", such as \"respond with 'not found' if the answer isn't present\""},{"key":"C","text":"Specify a structured output, such as (entity, relationship, entity) triples"},{"key":"D","text":"Ask a reasoning model to print its full chain of thought"},{"key":"E","text":"Rely only on a system message saying \"don't fabricate information\""}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-8.3-1","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.3 · Temperature, top_p and grounding","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about sampling parameters.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Higher temperature gives more random, divergent output; lower gives focused, concrete output. `top_p` also controls randomness in a different way, so adjust one at a time. Most reasoning models don't support `temperature` or `top_p` at all.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Temperature can be set between 0 and 2.","A low temperature such as 0.2 suits a legal document; a higher one such as 0.7 suits fiction.","The general recommendation is to change temperature or top_p, not both.","Reasoning models accept any temperature value."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-8.3-2","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.3 · Temperature, top_p and grounding","scenario":"","stem":"Your assistant must answer from up-to-date company policies. What does the prompt-engineering guide call the most effective way to get reliable answers?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"For anything that isn't purely creative, **grounding data** (relevant source material in the prompt) is the most effective route to reliable answers; the closer it is to the final answer's form, the less the model has to invent. RAG automates retrieving that grounding data.\n\n**Source:** [prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/prompt-engineering)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Provide grounding data in the prompt that the model draws its answer from"},{"key":"B","text":"Raise the temperature"},{"key":"C","text":"Use more few-shot examples of unrelated answers"},{"key":"D","text":"Ask the model to answer from memory"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-8.4-1","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.4 · System messages and safety system messages","scenario":"","stem":"Which statements about system messages are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"System messages (also called system prompts or metaprompts) define role and scope, tone, an output contract and safety constraints. They're **influence, not enforcement**: test them, make the fallback explicit (what to do out of scope or without information), and combine them with content filters and evaluations. They're optional but recommended.\n\n**Source:** [advanced prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/advanced-prompt-engineering) · [system message](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/system-message)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A system message influences the model but doesn't guarantee compliance"},{"key":"B","text":"You should layer system messages with other mitigations such as content filtering and evaluation"},{"key":"C","text":"System messages work best with unambiguous, non-conflicting rules and an explicit fallback behaviour"},{"key":"D","text":"A system message is required in every request"},{"key":"E","text":"A well-written system message replaces the need for testing"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-8.4-2","domain":3,"section":"8","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 8.4 · System messages and safety system messages","scenario":"","stem":"You're designing a **safety system message**. What does the guidance say you should build to evaluate it?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Treat the system message as a testable artifact: define the scenario, identify the RAI risks, decide boundary behaviour, **create a test set with benign and adversarial prompts** (to catch under-moderation, or \"leakage\"), then iterate, preferring the variant that reduces the most **severe** defects, not just the lowest overall defect rate.\n\n**Source:** [advanced prompt engineering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/advanced-prompt-engineering) · [system message](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/system-message)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A test set with both benign and adversarial prompts, preferring the version that reduces the most severe defects"},{"key":"B","text":"Nothing: safety system messages are validated by Microsoft"},{"key":"C","text":"Only benign prompts, so you measure helpfulness"},{"key":"D","text":"A single prompt that tries to jailbreak the model"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-9.1-1","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.1 · Guardrails, controls and intervention points","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Foundry guardrails.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Guardrails (previously content filters) use Azure AI Content Safety classifiers. Intervention points: **user input** and **output** for models and agents; **tool call** and **tool response** (preview) for agents only. Actions are *annotate* (models only) or *annotate and block*. New deployments get **Microsoft.DefaultV2**.\n\n**Source:** [guardrails overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A guardrail is a named collection of controls; each control defines a risk, where to scan for it and what to do.","Models can be scanned at the user input and output intervention points.","Tool call and tool response intervention points apply to models as well as agents.","By default, model deployments are assigned the `Microsoft.DefaultV2` guardrail."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-9.1-2","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.1 · Guardrails, controls and intervention points","scenario":"","stem":"A model deployment's guardrail blocks Violence at **High**. An agent built on that model has its own guardrail that sets Violence to **Low**. Which setting applies to the agent?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Risks in an agent are detected with **the agent's guardrail**, which fully overrides the model deployment's. If an agent has no guardrail of its own, it inherits the one on its model deployment; it only uses Microsoft.DefaultV2 when the deployment does or you assign it.\n\n**Source:** [guardrails overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The agent's guardrail: an agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's"},{"key":"B","text":"The model's, because it's stricter"},{"key":"C","text":"Both are combined, so the stricter one wins"},{"key":"D","text":"Neither; agents aren't filtered"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-9.1-3","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.1 · Guardrails, controls and intervention points","scenario":"","stem":"Which Azure OpenAI models are **not** covered by the content filtering system?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The content filtering system doesn't apply to prompts and completions processed by **embedding models** or **audio models such as Whisper**. It does apply to chat, reasoning and fine-tuned models in every deployment type.\n\n**Source:** [guardrails overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Embedding models and audio transcription models such as Whisper"},{"key":"B","text":"Reasoning models"},{"key":"C","text":"Fine-tuned models"},{"key":"D","text":"Models in Global Standard deployments"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-9.2-1","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.2 · Harm categories, severity and blocking levels","scenario":"","stem":"In a Foundry guardrail control for a harm category, match each **blocking level** to the severities it blocks.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Careful: the blocking level names the **threshold**, so *High* is the strictest. Content classified *safe* is annotated but never blocked. Anyone can turn harms off for inputs; turning them off for **outputs** requires approval for modified guardrails.\n\n**Source:** [content filter severity levels](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/content-filter-severity-levels) · [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Low","Medium","High","Off"],"values":["Low, medium and high severity (most restrictive)","Medium and high severity","Nothing is blocked; results can still be annotated","Only high-severity content (least restrictive)","Only safe content"],"answer":["Only high-severity content (least restrictive)","Medium and high severity","Low, medium and high severity (most restrictive)","Nothing is blocked; results can still be annotated"]}},{"id":"aoai-9.2-2","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.2 · Harm categories, severity and blocking levels","scenario":"","stem":"Which are the four core harm categories that the content filters classify by severity? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The four core categories are **hate and fairness, sexual, violence and self-harm**, each rated safe, low, medium or high in guardrails (Azure AI Content Safety itself uses a 0–7 scale for text, or 0/2/4/6 trimmed). Other risks, such as prompt attacks, protected material, PII and groundedness, are separate classifiers.\n\n**Source:** [content filter severity levels](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/content-filter-severity-levels) · [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Hate and fairness"},{"key":"B","text":"Sexual"},{"key":"C","text":"Violence"},{"key":"D","text":"Self-harm"},{"key":"E","text":"Profanity"},{"key":"F","text":"Misinformation"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-9.3-1","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.3 · Prompt Shields and protected material","scenario":"","stem":"Match each risk to what it detects.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Prompt Shields** cover user prompt attacks and document (indirect) attacks; they detect manipulation, not whether content is harmful, accurate or grounded. **Protected material** filters run on output; the code model might be required for Customer Copyright Commitment coverage.\n\n**Source:** [jailbreak detection](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection) · [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["User prompt attack (formerly jailbreak risk detection)","Indirect (document) attack","Protected material for text","Protected material for code"],"values":["A user prompt trying to bypass system instructions or safety training","Hidden instructions embedded in documents, emails or web pages fed to the model","Known text such as song lyrics, articles or recipes in the output","Output matching source code from public repositories","Whether the answer is factually true"],"answer":["A user prompt trying to bypass system instructions or safety training","Hidden instructions embedded in documents, emails or web pages fed to the model","Known text such as song lyrics, articles or recipes in the output","Output matching source code from public repositories"]}},{"id":"aoai-9.3-2","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.3 · Prompt Shields and protected material","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Prompt Shields in Foundry.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"User prompt attack detection is **on** by default; document attack detection is **off** and must be enabled in the guardrail controls. Indirect attacks are found in content marked with document delimiters. Each control returns `detected` and `filtered` flags, for example under `prompt_filter_results[].content_filter_results.jailbreak`.\n\n**Source:** [jailbreak detection](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection) · [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["User prompt attack detection is enabled by default.","Document attack detection is enabled by default.","Detecting indirect attacks requires using document delimiters when you build the prompt.","A detected but unblocked attack shows up as `detected: true, filtered: false` in the annotations."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-9.4-1","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.4 · Handling filtered requests in code","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about how filtering shows up in the API.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Filtered **prompts** fail with **400** (error code `content_filter`). Filtered **completions** return `finish_reason: content_filter` per affected choice; streaming stops at the filtered chunk. If the content filtering system is down, the request **still completes without filtering**; check `content_filter_results` for an error object such as `content_filter_error`.\n\n**Source:** [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A prompt classified at a filtered category and severity returns HTTP 400.","A non-streaming completion that's filtered has `finish_reason` set to `content_filter`.","With `n` greater than 1, only the filtered choices get `finish_reason: content_filter`.","If the filtering system is unavailable, the request fails."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-9.4-2","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.4 · Handling filtered requests in code","scenario":"","stem":"Your application must handle content filtering robustly. What should it do? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Handle both paths: an error for filtered input and `finish_reason: content_filter` for filtered output (possibly with partial content in long responses). Also verify the filters ran. Streaming output filters have a near-real-time mode that reduces latency.\n\n**Source:** [content filter](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/foundry-models/concepts/content-filter)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Catch HTTP 400 errors with code `content_filter` and show the user a helpful message"},{"key":"B","text":"Check each choice's `finish_reason` for `content_filter` before using the text"},{"key":"C","text":"Check `content_filter_results` for an error object showing the filters didn't run"},{"key":"D","text":"Retry filtered prompts with a higher temperature until they pass"},{"key":"E","text":"Assume every 200 response was filtered"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-9.5-1","domain":2,"section":"9","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 9.5 · Abuse monitoring","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about abuse monitoring.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Abuse monitoring combines content classification, abuse pattern capture, review (automated first, human when needed) and notification. Content filtering itself doesn't store prompts or use them for training without consent. Eligible customers with sensitive data could apply for **modified abuse monitoring**, but it won't be available for models released on or after November 1, 2026.\n\n**Source:** [abuse monitoring](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/abuse-monitoring)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Abuse monitoring looks for recurring content or behaviour that suggests the service is being used against the Code of Conduct.","Flagged prompts and completions are first reviewed by automated means, such as LLMs, and human review is only added in some cases.","Customers are normally told by email and given a chance to fix the behaviour before suspension, except for severe or recurring abuse.","Content filtering stores your prompts to retrain its classifiers."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-10.1-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.1 · The RAG pattern, and RAG versus fine-tuning","scenario":"","stem":"Put the three steps of the RAG flow in order.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"RAG = **retrieve → augment → generate**. An index (keyword, semantic, vector or hybrid) makes retrieval fast and consistent, and can store fields such as titles and URLs for better citations. The system message tells the model how to use the retrieved content.\n\n**Source:** [retrieval augmented generation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3"],"values":["Augment: combine the question and the retrieved grounding data into a prompt","Fine-tune: train the model on the retrieved content","Generate: the model answers from the augmented prompt, with citations","Retrieve: query an index or data store for content relevant to the question"],"answer":["Retrieve: query an index or data store for content relevant to the question","Augment: combine the question and the retrieved grounding data into a prompt","Generate: the model answers from the augmented prompt, with citations"]}},{"id":"aoai-10.1-2","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.1 · The RAG pattern, and RAG versus fine-tuning","scenario":"","stem":"Your support bot must answer from internal documents that change every week. Which approach do the Foundry docs recommend?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Use **RAG** for answers grounded in private or frequently changing data. Use **fine-tuning** to change behaviour, style or task performance, not to add fresh knowledge. Use **agent tools** (such as file search) when an agent needs retrieval as a tool.\n\n**Source:** [retrieval augmented generation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"RAG, because it grounds answers in private, frequently changing data"},{"key":"B","text":"Fine-tuning on the documents every week"},{"key":"C","text":"A larger context window with all documents in every prompt"},{"key":"D","text":"Raising the temperature"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-10.2-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.2 · Agentic retrieval and the On Your Data retirement","scenario":"","stem":"What does **agentic retrieval** add over a classic single-query RAG pipeline? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Agentic retrieval (agentic RAG) uses a model to break a complex input into focused subqueries, runs them **in parallel**, applies built-in semantic ranking, and returns **structured** grounding data, citations and metadata, optionally with a synthesized answer. Foundry IQ (Azure AI Search) is the recommended index store.\n\n**Source:** [retrieval augmented generation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation) · [use your data](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/use-your-data)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A model plans several focused subqueries using the conversation history"},{"key":"B","text":"The subqueries run in parallel"},{"key":"C","text":"It returns structured grounding data with citations and execution metadata"},{"key":"D","text":"It removes the need for any index"},{"key":"E","text":"It fine-tunes the chat model on your data"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-10.2-2","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.2 · Agentic retrieval and the On Your Data retirement","scenario":"","stem":"Your app still sends `data_sources` in its chat completions requests (Azure OpenAI On Your Data). Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"On Your Data (signalled by `data_sources`, `dataSources` or the `/extensions/chat/completions` path) **retired on 14 October 2026**; plain chat completions aren't affected. Migrate to Foundry IQ knowledge bases: reuse compatible indexes, enforce per-user authorization in your backend, keep conversation history in your app and validate citations. Connector behaviour, ACLs and embeddings don't convert automatically.\n\n**Source:** [retrieval augmented generation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation) · [use your data](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/use-your-data)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Azure OpenAI On Your Data retired on October 14, 2026.","Ordinary chat completions requests without On Your Data configuration are also retired.","You can reuse a compatible existing Azure AI Search index when you migrate to Foundry IQ.","Access control lists, connectors and embeddings convert automatically during migration."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-10.3-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.3 · Vector search in Azure AI Search","scenario":"","stem":"Which statements about vector search in Azure AI Search are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Vector search supports similarity, hybrid, multimodal, multilingual and filtered search, and can act as a vector database. A vector field isn't filterable, so filter on separate text or numeric fields. Embeddings come from **external processing** (push vectors) or **integrated vectorization**. Vector conversion is one-way, which is why indexes hold both vector and text fields.\n\n**Source:** [vector search overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It matches by meaning, so \"dog\" can find \"canine\" and the German \"Hund\""},{"key":"B","text":"A query can combine a vector query with a filter on filterable text or numeric fields"},{"key":"C","text":"You can push embeddings you computed yourself into vector fields, or let an indexer generate them"},{"key":"D","text":"Vector fields themselves are filterable"},{"key":"E","text":"Results come back as the vectors converted to text"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-10.4-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.4 · Hybrid search and Reciprocal Rank Fusion","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about hybrid search in Azure AI Search.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Hybrid search runs full-text (BM25) and vector (HNSW or exhaustive KNN) queries **in parallel** in one request, then merges them with **RRF**, which uses rank positions so the different score scales don't matter. Benchmarks show hybrid retrieval plus semantic ranker gives significant relevance gains.\n\n**Source:** [hybrid search overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A hybrid query is a single request with both `search` and `vectorQueries`.","The full-text and vector queries run one after the other.","Results are merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into one result set.","Hybrid queries can still use filters, facets, sorting and semantic ranking on text fields."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-10.5-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.5 · Semantic ranker","scenario":"","stem":"What does the **semantic ranker** in Azure AI Search do? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Semantic ranker adds **secondary ranking** over the top results (only the **top 50** go to the ranker), using deep-learning models adapted from Bing, plus **captions/answers** and optional **query rewrite**. It works on text, including the text portion of hybrid and vector queries. It's a premium, usage-billed feature with a free-tier allowance.\n\n**Source:** [semantic search overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-search-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Re-ranks an initial BM25 or RRF result set with Microsoft language-understanding models"},{"key":"B","text":"Returns captions, and optionally answers"},{"key":"C","text":"Can rewrite a query into several semantically similar queries"},{"key":"D","text":"Generates the embeddings for vector fields"},{"key":"E","text":"Re-ranks the entire index for every query"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-10.5-2","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.5 · Semantic ranker","scenario":"","stem":"Your semantic configuration lists `title`, `keywords` and `content` fields, and documents are very long. What happens to text beyond the ranker's input limit?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"For each document the ranker accepts up to about **2,000 tokens**: up to 128 for title, 128 for keywords, and the rest for content. Anything over the limit is trimmed, which is why field order in the semantic configuration matters.\n\n**Source:** [semantic search overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-search-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It's trimmed and ignored, so list fields in priority order"},{"key":"B","text":"It's split into extra results"},{"key":"C","text":"The query fails"},{"key":"D","text":"It's summarized by your chat deployment"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-10.6-1","domain":3,"section":"10","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 10.6 · Integrated vectorization","scenario":"","stem":"You use **integrated vectorization** in Azure AI Search. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Integrated vectorization needs an **indexer**, a supported data source, a **skillset** (a chunking skill such as Text Split, plus an embedding skill such as **AzureOpenAIEmbedding** for ada-002 or text-embedding-3) and an index. At query time, a **vectorizer** on the vector field (for example the Azure OpenAI vectorizer) encodes the text, and it **must match** the embedding model used for the content.\n\n**Source:** [vector search integrated vectorization](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-integrated-vectorization)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["An indexer and a skillset can chunk documents and call an Azure OpenAI embedding model during indexing.","A vectorizer in the index converts a text query to a vector at query time.","The query vectorizer can use a different embedding model from the one used to index the content.","Chunking is usually needed so documents fit the embedding model's input limit."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.1-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.1 · Customization methods: SFT, DPO and RFT","scenario":"","stem":"Match each fine-tuning method to the situation it fits.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SFT learns from prompts and desired responses; DPO from preferred vs rejected pairs; RFT from a grader's scores. Fine-tuning adjusts weights to improve accuracy, style, tool use or a smaller model's performance; it **doesn't replace retrieval** for current information. Managed Training runs these for you; Programmed Training (preview) lets you write the training loop in Python.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/fine-tuning/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)","Direct preference optimization (DPO)","Reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT)"],"values":["Many answers are possible and a grader can reliably score them","Quality depends on preferences such as tone, shown as a preferred and a rejected response to the same input","You can demonstrate the desired response for each prompt","You need the model to know this week's prices"],"answer":["You can demonstrate the desired response for each prompt","Quality depends on preferences such as tone, shown as a preferred and a rejected response to the same input","Many answers are possible and a grader can reliably score them"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.1-2","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.1 · Customization methods: SFT, DPO and RFT","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about fine-tuning in Foundry.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Both training approaches run on Foundry-managed infrastructure. Training types (for example Global, Standard, Data Zone) decide where training runs; **Global** is cheaper but has no data-residency guarantee. Deployment is separate: **serverless** deployment types, or **managed compute** with LoRA adapters (preview). Fine-tuning adds training and hosting costs, so compare against your baseline on held-out data.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/fine-tuning/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Fine-tuning can reduce prompt overhead because you need fewer examples in each prompt.","You must provision your own training GPUs.","Global training costs less per token but doesn't offer data residency.","A fine-tuned model can be served serverless (with deployment types such as Global Standard or Developer) or on managed compute."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.2-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.2 · Running a supervised fine-tuning job","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the steps of a supervised fine-tuning job with the OpenAI SDK.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SFT data is `.jsonl` in the chat **messages** format, ending with the desired **assistant** response; keep separate training and validation files. Upload with `purpose=\"fine-tune\"`. Hyperparameters such as `n_epochs`, `batch_size` and `learning_rate_multiplier` go under `method.supervised.hyperparameters` (`auto` lets the service choose). List events and **checkpoints** to compare candidates; don't assume the latest checkpoint is best.\n\n**Source:** [fine tuning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/fine-tuning)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"# training.jsonl: one conversation per line\n{\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"...\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"},\n              {\"role\": \"[[0]]\", \"content\": \"the desired answer\"}]}\n\ntrain, valid = [client.files.create(file=open(f, \"rb\"), purpose=\"[[1]]\")\n                for f in (\"training.jsonl\", \"validation.jsonl\")]\n\njob = client.fine_tuning.jobs.create(\n    model=\"<base model>\", training_file=train.id, validation_file=valid.id,\n    method={\"type\": \"supervised\",\n            \"supervised\": {\"hyperparameters\": {\"[[2]]\": \"auto\"}}})\n\nfor cp in client.fine_tuning.jobs.[[3]].list(job.id): print(cp)","values":["assistant","batch","checkpoints","epochs_count","events_only","fine-tune","n_epochs","user"],"answer":["assistant","fine-tune","n_epochs","checkpoints"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.2-2","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.2 · Running a supervised fine-tuning job","scenario":"","stem":"Match each fine-tuning hyperparameter or metric to its meaning.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Start with the model's default hyperparameters, then compare validation results before changing them. Watch training loss (should fall), mean token accuracy (should rise) and the full validation metrics at each epoch. Cancelling training doesn't delete deployments or stop their charges.\n\n**Source:** [fine tuning](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/fine-tuning)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Number of epochs","Batch size","Learning rate multiplier","Validation loss rising while training loss falls"],"values":["A sign of overfitting: inspect the failing validation examples","Complete passes through the training data; more can overfit","Examples processed together per update; larger means fewer, lower-variance updates","Scales the learning rate; smaller can reduce overfitting","The number of GPUs used"],"answer":["Complete passes through the training data; more can overfit","Examples processed together per update; larger means fewer, lower-variance updates","Scales the learning rate; smaller can reduce overfitting","A sign of overfitting: inspect the failing validation examples"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.3-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.3 · Monitoring with Azure Monitor","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about monitoring Azure OpenAI with Azure Monitor.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Metrics and the activity log are automatic; **resource logs need a diagnostic setting** (with extra cost for Azure Monitor Logs). In Log Analytics, query the `AzureDiagnostics` and `AzureMetrics` tables. The built-in dashboards cover HTTP Requests, Tokens-Based Usage, PTU Utilization and Fine-tuning.\n\n**Source:** [monitor openai](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/monitor-openai)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Platform metrics and the activity log are collected automatically.","Resource logs are collected automatically without any configuration.","A diagnostic setting can route logs to Log Analytics, a storage account or Event Hubs.","For response-time investigations, the docs recommend the newer latency metrics over the legacy `Latency` metric."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-11.4-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.4 · Evaluation, tracing and observability","scenario":"","stem":"Which are the three core observability capabilities in Microsoft Foundry? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Evaluation** measures quality and safety with built-in evaluators (for example coherence, fluency, groundedness, relevance) or custom ones; AI-assisted evaluators use a judge model. **Monitoring** in production uses Application Insights dashboards and alerts. **Tracing** is built on **OpenTelemetry** and captures LLM calls, tool calls and agent decisions.\n\n**Source:** [observability](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/observability)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Evaluation"},{"key":"B","text":"Monitoring"},{"key":"C","text":"Tracing"},{"key":"D","text":"Fine-tuning"},{"key":"E","text":"Quota management"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-11.4-2","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.4 · Evaluation, tracing and observability","scenario":"","stem":"Before going to production, you want to probe your agent for safety and security weaknesses with simulated attacks. Which Foundry tool fits?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The **AI red teaming agent** simulates complex attacks with **PyRIT** to find safety and security vulnerabilities before deployment, ideally with humans in the loop. The lifecycle has three evaluation stages: base model selection (benchmarks), pre-production evaluation, and post-production monitoring.\n\n**Source:** [observability](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/concepts/observability)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The AI red teaming agent, which uses Microsoft's PyRIT framework"},{"key":"B","text":"The capacity calculator"},{"key":"C","text":"Stored completions"},{"key":"D","text":"Global Batch"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-11.5-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 11.5 · Foundry Agent Service","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the Foundry Agent Service option.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Prompt agents** are declarative and Foundry runs them. **Hosted agents** bring your own code (Agent Framework, LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK and more) as a container image or a .zip that Foundry builds. Calling the **Responses API** directly creates an **ephemeral agent** defined in your code, with no agent resource in Foundry. **Voice-based prompt agents** connect through Voice Live over WebSocket.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Instructions, a model and tools, with no code or infrastructure to manage","Your own LangGraph code with a managed endpoint, scaling and identity","Agent code already running elsewhere that just needs Foundry models and tools","A real-time spoken experience without hosting voice orchestration"],"values":["Call the Responses API (an ephemeral agent)","Global Batch job","Hosted agent","Prompt agent","Voice-based prompt agent"],"answer":["Prompt agent","Hosted agent","Call the Responses API (an ephemeral agent)","Voice-based prompt agent"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.1-1","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.1 · Credentials for Azure OpenAI in the database","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the two database scoped credentials: the first authenticates to Azure OpenAI with an API key, the second with the server's managed identity.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**`HTTPEndpointHeaders`** adds the flat JSON in `SECRET` as request headers; Azure OpenAI's key header is `api-key`. **`Managed Identity`** sends the server's managed identity token, with the resource ID in `SECRET`. Other identities are `HTTPEndpointQueryString` and `Shared Access Signature`. Credentials need a database master key.\n\n**Source:** [create external model transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql) · [sp invoke external rest endpoint transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-endpoint-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"CREATE DATABASE SCOPED CREDENTIAL [https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/]\n    WITH IDENTITY = '[[0]]',\n    SECRET = '{\"[[1]]\":\"<key>\"}';\n\nCREATE DATABASE SCOPED CREDENTIAL [https://my-aoai2.openai.azure.com/]\n    WITH IDENTITY = '[[2]]',\n    SECRET = '{\"resourceid\":\"https://cognitiveservices.azure.com\"}';","options":[["HTTPEndpointHeaders","HTTPEndpointQueryString","Shared Access Signature"],["Bearer","Ocp-Apim-Key","api-key"],["HTTPEndpointHeaders","Managed Identity","SYSTEM_ASSIGNED"]],"answer":["HTTPEndpointHeaders","api-key","Managed Identity"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.1-2","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.1 · Credentials for Azure OpenAI in the database","scenario":"","stem":"A credential named `[https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/]` exists. Answer Yes or No for each statement about the credential naming rules.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The credential name must be a valid URL **without a query string**, on an allowed domain, whose protocol + FQDN matches the called URL, and whose path is **the same or more generic** than the request path. A credential for a deeper path can't be used for a shorter URL. Scheme and host compare case-insensitively; the rest is case-sensitive.\n\n**Source:** [create external model transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql) · [sp invoke external rest endpoint transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-endpoint-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["It can be used for a call to `https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/emb/embeddings`.","It can be used for a call to `https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/`.","A credential name can include a query string.","The protocol and fully qualified domain name of the called URL must match those of the credential name."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.1-3","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.1 · Credentials for Azure OpenAI in the database","scenario":"","stem":"SQL Server 2025 (on-premises, enabled by Azure Arc) must call Azure OpenAI with its managed identity. Which steps are required?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"On SQL Server 2025 the managed identity comes from **Azure Arc**. Enable `allow server scoped db credentials` with `sp_configure` (requires ALTER SETTINGS), and grant the Arc identity an Azure OpenAI role (the external model docs specify **Cognitive Services OpenAI Contributor**). Azure SQL Database and Managed Instance use their own managed identities.\n\n**Source:** [create external model transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql) · [sp invoke external rest endpoint transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-endpoint-transact-sql)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Connect the server to Azure Arc and enable its primary managed identity, run `sp_configure 'allow server scoped db credentials', 1`, and grant the identity a role on the Azure OpenAI resource"},{"key":"B","text":"Store the API key in a `Managed Identity` credential"},{"key":"C","text":"Nothing: SQL Server always has a managed identity"},{"key":"D","text":"Create a contained database user for the Azure OpenAI resource"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-12.2-1","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.2 · CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the statement that registers an Azure OpenAI embedding deployment as an external model, returning 1,536 dimensions and retrying transient errors 3 times.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`API_FORMAT` accepts `Azure OpenAI`, `OpenAI`, `Ollama` and `ONNX Runtime`; `MODEL_TYPE` currently accepts only **`EMBEDDINGS`**. `PARAMETERS` is JSON appended to every request body (for example `dimensions`), and `sql_rest_options.retry_count` (0–10) retries transient HTTP errors. The Azure OpenAI location format is `https://{endpoint}/openai/deployments/{deployment}/embeddings?api-version={date}`, and it must be HTTPS.\n\n**Source:** [create external model transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL MyEmbeddings\nWITH (\n    LOCATION   = 'https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/emb3/embeddings?api-version=2024-02-01',\n    API_FORMAT = '[[0]]',\n    MODEL_TYPE = [[1]],\n    MODEL      = 'text-embedding-3-small',\n    CREDENTIAL = [https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/],\n    PARAMETERS = '[[2]]'\n);","values":["Azure OpenAI","CHAT","EMBEDDINGS","ONNX Runtime","OpenAI","{\"dimensions\":1536, \"sql_rest_options\":{\"retry_count\":3}}","{\"retry_count\":3, \"size\":1536}"],"answer":["Azure OpenAI","EMBEDDINGS","{\"dimensions\":1536, \"sql_rest_options\":{\"retry_count\":3}}"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.2-2","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.2 · CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the permission statement.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Creating or altering external models needs `CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL` or `ALTER ANY EXTERNAL MODEL`. Using a model in an AI function needs **`EXECUTE ON EXTERNAL MODEL::name`**. Calling the REST procedure needs **`EXECUTE ANY EXTERNAL ENDPOINT`**. Model metadata is in `sys.external_models`.\n\n**Source:** [create external model transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["A developer creates external models","A developer changes any external model","An app user generates embeddings with `MyEmbeddings`","An app user calls `sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint`"],"values":["GRANT ALTER ANY EXTERNAL MODEL TO dev;","GRANT CONTROL SERVER TO app;","GRANT CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL TO dev;","GRANT EXECUTE ANY EXTERNAL ENDPOINT TO app;","GRANT EXECUTE ON EXTERNAL MODEL::MyEmbeddings TO app;"],"answer":["GRANT CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL TO dev;","GRANT ALTER ANY EXTERNAL MODEL TO dev;","GRANT EXECUTE ON EXTERNAL MODEL::MyEmbeddings TO app;","GRANT EXECUTE ANY EXTERNAL ENDPOINT TO app;"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.3-1","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.3 · AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS and AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about `AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS`.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(text USE MODEL m [PARAMETERS json])` calls the external model and returns a JSON float array. Runtime parameters override the model's. It needs `sp_configure 'external rest endpoint enabled', 1` on SQL Server 2025; this option is **on by default** in Azure SQL Database and SQL database in Fabric. The `ai_generate_embeddings_summary` extended event helps troubleshooting.\n\n**Source:** [ai generate embeddings transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-generate-embeddings-transact-sql) · [ai generate chunks transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-generate-chunks-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Its syntax is `AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(source USE MODEL model_name)`.","It returns the embedding as a JSON array that you can store in a `VECTOR` column.","Its optional `PARAMETERS` JSON overrides the same setting in the model definition, such as `dimensions`, for that call.","On SQL Server 2025 it works without enabling `external rest endpoint enabled`."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.3-2","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.3 · AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS and AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS","scenario":"","stem":"You need to split long documents into 500-character pieces with 10% overlap before embedding them in T-SQL. Which call does that?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS` is a **table-valued** function (use `CROSS APPLY`). `CHUNK_TYPE` is currently only `FIXED`; `CHUNK_SIZE` is in characters and `OVERLAP` is a **percentage** of the chunk size. It returns `chunk`, `chunk_order`, `chunk_offset`, `chunk_length` (and `chunk_set_id` with `ENABLE_CHUNK_SET_ID = 1`), and needs compatibility level 170.\n\n**Source:** [ai generate embeddings transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-generate-embeddings-transact-sql) · [ai generate chunks transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-generate-chunks-transact-sql)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`SELECT c.chunk, c.chunk_order FROM dbo.Docs AS d CROSS APPLY AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS(SOURCE = d.Body, CHUNK_TYPE = FIXED, CHUNK_SIZE = 500, OVERLAP = 10) AS c;`"},{"key":"B","text":"`SELECT AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS(d.Body, 500, 10) FROM dbo.Docs AS d;`"},{"key":"C","text":"`SELECT * FROM STRING_SPLIT(d.Body, 500);`"},{"key":"D","text":"`SELECT AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(d.Body USE MODEL MyEmbeddings PARAMETERS '{\"chunk\":500}')`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"aoai-12.4-1","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.4 · Chat completions with sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the T-SQL that sends a chat completions request to Azure OpenAI and reads the answer.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint` takes `@url` (HTTPS only), `@method` (default `POST`), `@headers`, `@payload`, `@timeout`, `@credential` and **`@response … OUTPUT`**. The response JSON has **`$.response`** (HTTP status and headers) and **`$.result`** (the service's body), so the model's text is at `$.result.choices[0].message.content`. The return code is 0 for a 2xx status, otherwise the HTTP status code.\n\n**Source:** [sp invoke external rest endpoint transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-endpoint-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"DECLARE @payload nvarchar(max) = JSON_OBJECT('messages': JSON_ARRAY(\n        JSON_OBJECT('role':'system', 'content':'Answer only from the context.'),\n        JSON_OBJECT('role':'user',   'content':@question)));\nDECLARE @response nvarchar(max), @rc int;\n\nEXEC @rc = sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint\n     @url        = N'https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/chat/chat/completions?api-version=2024-10-21',\n     @method     = '[[0]]',\n     @credential = [https://my-aoai.openai.azure.com/],\n     @payload    = @payload,\n     @response   = @response [[1]];\n\nSELECT JSON_VALUE(@response, '[[2]]') AS answer;","values":["$.choices[0].text","$.response.choices[0].message.content","$.result.choices[0].message.content","GET","OUT","OUTPUT","POST"],"answer":["POST","OUTPUT","$.result.choices[0].message.content"]}},{"id":"aoai-12.4-2","domain":3,"section":"12","source":"aoai","sourceLabel":"Azure OpenAI 12.4 · Chat completions with sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about `sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint`.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Only endpoints on an **allowed list** of Azure services can be called (Azure OpenAI, Azure AI Services, Functions, Logic Apps, Event Hubs, Blob Storage, Microsoft Graph, API Management and more); front anything else with **API Management**. Limits include 100 MB payloads, an 8 KB URL and 8 KB of headers. Concurrent outbound connections are capped, raising error 10928 when exceeded.\n\n**Source:** [sp invoke external rest endpoint transact sql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-endpoint-transact-sql)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["It can call `*.openai.azure.com` and `*.cognitiveservices.azure.com` endpoints.","It can call any public HTTPS endpoint on the internet.","To reach a service that isn't on the allowed list, you can expose it through Azure API Management.","Request and response payloads are limited to 100 MB."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}}]};
